angle, and its sine has the value\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡C=\\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡C & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡C} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186083644 1070 17592186045485 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186083644 1071 "$\\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡{45}^{∘}=$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186083644 1071 "What is $\\sin 45^{∘} - \\cos 45^{∘}$?" 13194139535791 17592186045417 false] [17592186083644 1074 "The angle ${45}^{∘}$ is a special angle, and its trigonometric ratios have the special values\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡{45}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡{45}^{∘} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & = \\\\ 0 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186083653 1070 17592186045487 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186083653 1071 "$\\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡{90}^{∘}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}=$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186083653 1071 "What is $\\sin 45^{∘} + \\sin 90^{∘} + \\sin 30^{∘}$?" 13194139535791 17592186045417 false] [17592186083653 1074 "The angles ${45}^{∘},$ ${90}^{∘}$ and ${30}^{∘}$ are special angles, and their trigonometric ratios have the values\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡{90}^{∘}=1,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡{90}^{∘}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+1+\\frac{1}{2} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}+2+1}{2} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}+3}{2}. & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186084153 1061 2109 13194139535791 17592186045417 false] [17592186084153 1061 623442 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186084154 1061 2110 13194139535791 17592186045417 false] [17592186084154 1061 623443 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186084155 1061 2114 13194139535791 17592186045417 false] [17592186084155 1061 623450 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186084201 1061 23435 13194139535791 17592186045417 false] [17592186084201 1061 623447 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186084203 1061 23462 13194139535791 17592186045417 false] [17592186084203 1061 623454 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186084207 1061 23530 13194139535791 17592186045417 false] [17592186084207 1061 623446 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186084412 1064 "Let's take a look at the isosceles right triangle whose legs are both equal to $1$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/a6/a6d1e8bd928fe666859f7101be65819af5f16fe81df3900941650d279eecd496.png)\n\nBy considering either of the $45^\\circ$ angles in this triangle, we find that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\sin 45^\\circ &= \\dfrac{\\textrm{opp.}}{\\textrm{hyp.}} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}, \\\\[5pt] \\cos 45^\\circ &= \\dfrac{\\textrm{adj.}}{\\textrm{hyp.}} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}, \\\\[5pt] \\tan 45^\\circ &= \\dfrac{\\textrm{opp.}}{\\textrm{adj.}} = \\dfrac{1}{1} = 1. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIsosceles right triangles are very common, so the values above occur a lot in trigonometry. It's a good idea to remember them." 13194139535791 17592186045417 false] [17592186084412 1064 "Let's take a look at the isosceles right triangle whose legs are both equal to $1,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/a6/a6d1e8bd928fe666859f7101be65819af5f16fe81df3900941650d279eecd496.png)\n\nBy considering either of the $45^\\circ$ angles in this triangle, we find that\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\\sin 45^\\circ &= \\dfrac{\\textrm{opp.}}{\\textrm{hyp.}} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}, \\[5pt]\n\t\\cos 45^\\circ &= \\dfrac{\\textrm{adj.}}{\\textrm{hyp.}} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}, \\[5pt]\n\t\\tan 45^\\circ &= \\dfrac{\\textrm{opp.}}{\\textrm{adj.}} = \\dfrac{1}{1} = 1. \n}\n$$\n\nIsosceles right triangles are very common, so the values above occur a lot in trigonometry. It's a good idea to remember them." 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186084413 1064 "Let's now consider the $30$-$60$-$90$ triangle whose shortest leg has a length equal to $1$, shown below.\n\n![](images/67/679ce670484e57ba699030de4cf6d9ba52a5d6910d22b4beb2ad5d4e8ac4e0e0.png)\n\nNow, we can calculate some trigonometric ratios without a calculator. Let's start with the $30^\\circ$-angle. By the definition of sine, cosine, and tangent, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin 30^{∘} &= \\frac{opp.}{hyp.} = \\frac{1}{2}, \\\\\n\\cos 30^{∘} &= \\frac{adj.}{hyp.} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}, \\\\\n\\tan 30^{∘} &= \\frac{opp.}{adj.} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, by considering the $60^\\circ$-angle, we find that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin 60^{∘} &= \\frac{opp.}{hyp.} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}, \\\\\n\\cos 60^{∘} &= \\frac{adj.}{hyp.} = \\frac{1}{2}, \\\\\n\\tan 60^{∘} &= \\frac{opp.}{adj.} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{1} = \\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 false] [17592186084413 1064 "Let's now consider the $30$-$60$-$90$ triangle whose shortest leg has a length equal to $1,$ shown below.\n\n![](images/67/679ce670484e57ba699030de4cf6d9ba52a5d6910d22b4beb2ad5d4e8ac4e0e0.png)\n\nNow, we can calculate some trigonometric ratios without a calculator. Let's start with the $30^\\circ$-angle. By the definition of sine, cosine, and tangent, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘} & =\\frac{\\text{opp.}}{\\text{hyp.}}=\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘} & =\\frac{\\text{adj.}}{\\text{hyp.}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}, \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡{30}^{∘} & =\\frac{\\text{opp.}}{\\text{adj.}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, by considering the $60^\\circ$-angle, we find that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{60}^{∘} & =\\frac{\\text{opp.}}{\\text{hyp.}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}, \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡{60}^{∘} & =\\frac{\\text{adj.}}{\\text{hyp.}}=\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡{60}^{∘} & =\\frac{\\text{opp.}}{\\text{adj.}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{1}=\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186084414 1064 "The values of the trigonometric ratios for $0^\\circ$ angles are the following:\n\n| Trigonometric ratio | Value |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\sin \\left(0^\\circ\\right)$ | $0$ |\n| $\\cos \\left(0^\\circ\\right)$ | $1$ |\n| $\\tan \\left(0^\\circ\\right)$ | $0$ |\n\nWhy is this so? If we look at the right triangle below, we can imagine making the angle $\\theta$ smaller by moving the vertex $C$ towards $A.$ \n\n![](images/9b/9bee0468c989067e12648e538788bcd8c7fb879e112000a942a1ee6f41d95c7d.png)\n\nAs the angle $\\theta$ gets closer and closer to zero, the opposite side $AC$ also approaches zero. Also, the hypotenuse $BC$ and the adjacent side $AB$ become nearly the same length.\n\nSo, as $\\theta$ approaches zero (which we can write as $\\theta \\rightarrow 0^\\circ$) we have\n\n$$\nAC \\approx 0 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad BC \\approx AB.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, when $\\theta = 0^\\circ$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({0}^{∘}) & =\\frac{AC}{BC}=0, \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡({0}^{∘}) & =\\frac{AB}{BC}=1, \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡({0}^{∘}) & =\\frac{AC}{AB}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186084414 1064 "The values of the trigonometric ratios for $0^\\circ$ angles are the following:\n\n| Trigonometric ratio | Value |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $\\sin \\left(0^\\circ\\right)$ | $0$ |\n| $\\cos \\left(0^\\circ\\right)$ | $1$ |\n| $\\tan \\left(0^\\circ\\right)$ | $0$ |\n\nWhy is this so? If we look at the right triangle below, we can imagine making the angle $\\theta$ smaller by moving the vertex $C$ towards $A$.\n\n![](images/9b/9bee0468c989067e12648e538788bcd8c7fb879e112000a942a1ee6f41d95c7d.png)\n\nAs the angle $\\theta$ gets closer and closer to zero, the opposite side $AC$ also approaches zero. Also, the hypotenuse $BC$ and the adjacent side $AB$ become nearly the same length.\n\nSo, as $\\theta$ approaches zero (which we can write as $\\theta \\rightarrow 0^\\circ$) we have\n\n$$\nAC \\approx 0 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad BC \\approx AB\n$$\n\nTherefore, when\n\n$$\n\\theta = 0^\\circ\n$$\n\nwe have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin (0^{∘}) &= \\frac{AC}{BC} = 0, \\\\\n\\cos (0^{∘}) &= \\frac{AB}{BC} = 1, \\\\\n\\tan (0^{∘}) &= \\frac{AC}{AB} = 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 false] [17592186084415 1064 "The values of the trigonometric ratios for $90^\\circ$ angles are the following:\n\n| Trigonometric ratio | Value |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\sin \\left(90^\\circ\\right)$ | $1$ |\n| $\\cos \\left(90^\\circ\\right)$ | $0$ |\n| $\\tan \\left(90^\\circ\\right)$ | Undefined |\n\nTo understand where the values come from, this time, we can imagine increasing the value of the angle $\\theta$ by moving the vertex $B$ towards $A.$ \n\n![](images/53/53d538ce2eca6af579b556bdac72d9fba9ba5e817de38ff73236a312b401c4cf.png)\n\nAs $B$ moves towards $A,$ the adjacent side $AB$ gets very close to zero, and the angle $\\theta$ gets very close to $90^\\circ.$ Also, the hypotenuse $BC$ and the opposite side $AC$ become nearly the same length.\n\nSo, as $\\theta$ approaches $90^\\circ$ (which we can write as $\\theta \\rightarrow 90^\\circ$) we have\n\n$$\nAB \\approx 0 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad BC \\approx AC.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, when $\\theta = 90^\\circ,$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({90}^{∘}) & =\\frac{AC}{BC}=1, \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡({90}^{∘}) & =\\frac{AB}{BC}=0, \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡({90}^{∘}) & =\\frac{AC}{AB}=\\frac{AC}{0}=\\text{undefined.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when calculating the tangent, we get a $0$ in the denominator. We know that we can't divide by zero. Therefore, the tangent of a right angle is said to be undefined." 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186084415 1064 "The values of the trigonometric ratios for $90^\\circ$ angles are the following:\n\n| Trigonometric ratio | Value |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $\\sin \\left(90^\\circ\\right)$ | $1$ |\n| $\\cos \\left(90^\\circ\\right)$ | $0$ |\n| $\\tan \\left(90^\\circ\\right)$ | Undefined |\n\nTo understand where the values come from, this time, we can imagine increasing the value of the angle $\\theta$ by moving the vertex $B$ towards $A$.\n\n![](images/53/53d538ce2eca6af579b556bdac72d9fba9ba5e817de38ff73236a312b401c4cf.png)\n\nAs $B$ moves towards $A$, the adjacent side $AB$ gets very close to zero, and the angle $\\theta$ gets very close to $90^\\circ$. Also, the hypotenuse $BC$ and the opposite side $AC$ become nearly the same length.\n\nSo, as $\\theta$ approaches $90^\\circ$ (which we can write as $\\theta \\rightarrow 90^\\circ$) we have\n\n$$\nAB \\approx 0 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad BC \\approx AC\n$$\n\nTherefore, when\n\n$$\n\\theta = 90^\\circ\n$$\n\nwe have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin (90^{∘}) &= \\frac{AC}{BC} = 1, \\\\\n\\cos (90^{∘}) &= \\frac{AB}{BC} = 0, \\\\\n\\tan (90^{∘}) &= \\frac{AC}{AB} = \\frac{AC}{0} = undefined\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when calculating the tangent, we get a $0$ in the denominator. We know that we can't divide by zero. Therefore, the tangent of a right angle is said to be undefined." 13194139535791 17592186045417 false] [17592186096477 1074 "To obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)$ from $y=f(x)$, we need to reflect the graph over the $x$-axis. The resulting graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/c9/c978264dfc9cc8f492c3b984ed93273f508c6a25fb82592dd70c4dfe642a8b19.png)" 13194139535791 17592186045417 false] [17592186096477 1074 "To obtain the graph of $y=−f(x)$ from $y=f(x)$, we need to reflect the graph over the $x$-axis. The resulting graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/c9/c978264dfc9cc8f492c3b984ed93273f508c6a25fb82592dd70c4dfe642a8b19.png)" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186096478 1071 "The point $P$ with coordinates $(2, -1)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x)$. Which point must lie on the graph of $y= -f(x)$?" 13194139535791 17592186045417 false] [17592186096478 1071 "The point $P$ with coordinates $(2, -1)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x)$. Which point must lie on the graph of $y= -f(x)?$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186096478 1074 "The transformation $y=-f(x)$ reflects the curve $y=f(x)$ in the $x$-axis.\n\nFirst, notice that the point $P(2, -1)$ lies $1$ unit *below* the $x$-axis. So, under the action of the transformation, this point will be reflected $1$ unit *above* the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/5f/5fc11d4ceb5ce0a1c9e7cd95fed19a07b3b3a78185f7dd8131f069bcdcc4bb1a.png)\n\nWe conclude that the point $(2,1)$ must lie on the graph of $y=-f(x)$." 13194139535791 17592186045417 false] [17592186096478 1074 "The transformation $y=-f(x)$ reflects the curve $y=f(x)$ in the $x$-axis.\n\nFirst, notice that the point $P(2, -1)$ lies $1$ unit *below* the $x$-axis. So, under the action of the transformation, this point will be reflected $1$ unit *above* the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/5f/5fc11d4ceb5ce0a1c9e7cd95fed19a07b3b3a78185f7dd8131f069bcdcc4bb1a.png)\n\nWe conclude that the point $(2,1)$ must lie on the graph of $y=-f(x).$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186096685 1038 17592186527977 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186096685 1038 17592186527978 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186096686 1038 17592186527913 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186096686 1038 17592186527955 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186096686 1038 17592186527969 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186096720 1038 17592186527967 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186096731 1038 17592186527964 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186096765 1038 17592186527958 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186096821 1038 17592186527920 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186096917 1050 420.0 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186097846 1064 "The graph of $y=f(x)$ shown below. How can we plot the graph of $y=-f(x)$?\n\n![](images/5b/5bab57ba6ac7cd9b0ea80287457ddc7ee50cadf2580a84a802fa039bbca27e1a.png)\n\nAs usual, It's helpful to first find a few points on the graph of $y=-f(x)$. You can use the points on the graph of $y=f(x)$ to identify corresponding points on the graph of $y=-f(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Points on }y=f(x) & \\text{ Points on }y=-f(x) \\\\ (-1,2) & (-1,-2) \\\\ (0,-1) & (0,1) \\\\ (2,0) & (2,0) \\\\ (3,4) & (3,-4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor each point on the graph of $y=f(x)$ there is a point on the graph of $y=-f(x)$ with the *same* $x$-value and the *opposite* $y$-value. \n\nLet's plot our new points, and draw $y=-f(x)$.\n\n![](images/5a/5a1e6c5a95dff6b8e1f7eba5de197ba61f0ae64a94db2eeb57fc6cd23661f877.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=-f(x)$ is a **reflection across the $x$-axis** of the graph of $y=f(x)$." 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186097846 1064 "The graph of $y=f(x)$ shown below. How can we plot the graph of $y=-f(x)$?\n\n![](images/5b/5bab57ba6ac7cd9b0ea80287457ddc7ee50cadf2580a84a802fa039bbca27e1a.png)\n\nAs usual, It's helpful to first find a few points on the graph of $y=-f(x)$. You can use the points on the graph of $y=f(x)$ to identify corresponding points on the graph of $y=-f(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}Points ony = f(x) & Points ony =-f(x) \\\\ (-1, 2) & (-1,-2) \\\\ (0,-1) & (0, 1) \\\\ (2, 0) & (2, 0) \\\\ (3, 4) & (3,-4)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFor each point on the graph of $y=f(x)$ there is a point on the graph of $y=-f(x)$ with the *same* $x$-value and the *opposite* $y$-value.\n\nLet's plot our new points, and draw $y=-f(x)$.\n\n![](images/5a/5a1e6c5a95dff6b8e1f7eba5de197ba61f0ae64a94db2eeb57fc6cd23661f877.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=-f(x)$ is a **reflection across the $x$-axis** of the graph of $y=f(x)$." 13194139535791 17592186045417 false] [17592186099909 1078 17592186099914 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186100932 1038 17592186527970 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186101458 1070 17592186045486 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186101458 1071 "Calculate\n$∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{1}{n^{2} + 5n + 6}$.\n\n*Hint: Try to factor the denominator first.*" 13194139535791 17592186045417 false] [17592186101458 1071 "Calculate \n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}+5n+6}.$\n\n*Hint: Try to factor the denominator first.*" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186101458 1074 "First, factoring the denominator and using partial fractions, we can write the series as\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}+5n+6}=\\frac{1}{(n+2)(n+3)}=\\frac{1}{n+2}-\\frac{1}{n+3}.\n$$\n\nSo, our series can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(\\frac{1}{n+2}-\\frac{1}{n+3}).\n$$\n\nLet's now compute the first few partial sums:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{1} & =(\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\ {s}_{2} & =(\\frac{1}{3}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}}-\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\ {s}_{3} & =(\\frac{1}{3}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}}-\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\ {s}_{4} & =(\\frac{1}{3}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}}-\\frac{1}{7})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that for each partial sum, we always end up canceling all terms except for the first and last terms. Therefore, the $N$th partial sum is\n\n$$\n{s}_{N}=\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{N+3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can determine the sum of the series by taking the limit of ${s}_{N}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(n+2)(n+3)} & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }{s}_{N} \\\\  & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{N+3}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186105923 1038 17592186527936 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186119859 1038 17592186527961 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186130924 1078 17592186130927 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186133891 1038 17592186527992 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186133892 1038 17592186527925 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186133893 1038 17592186527980 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186133914 1038 17592186527982 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186133914 1038 17592186528005 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186133915 1038 17592186527912 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186133915 1038 17592186527988 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186133916 1038 17592186527911 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186133916 1038 17592186527918 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186133916 1038 17592186527962 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186133917 1038 17592186527996 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186133917 1038 17592186528004 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186133917 1038 17592186528008 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186133922 1038 17592186527910 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186133922 1038 17592186528011 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186133923 1038 17592186527914 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186133923 1038 17592186527957 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186133923 1038 17592186528006 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186133924 1038 17592186527947 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186133924 1038 17592186527997 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186133925 1038 17592186527916 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186133925 1038 17592186527928 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186133925 1038 17592186527935 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186133925 1038 17592186527974 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186133995 1050 900.0 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186134014 1050 1200.0 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186134015 1050 960.0 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186134128 1070 17592186045487 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186134128 1071 "Evaluate the mixed integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{y{z}^{2}}\\frac{12}{z}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ in the $yz$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 2,\\,1\\le z\\le \\sqrt{2y}\\}.\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186134128 1071 "Evaluate the mixed integral\n$∬_(D)[\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{yz^{2}}\\frac{12}{z}dx]dA$\nwhere the region $D$ in the $yz$-plane is given by\n$D = {(y, z): 1 \\le y \\le 2, 1 \\le z \\le \\sqrt{2y}}$." 13194139535791 17592186045417 false] [17592186134128 1074 "First, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $x,$ treating $y$ and $z$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{y{z}^{2}}\\frac{12}{z}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{12}{z}[x]_{x=0}^{x=y{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{12}{z}(y{z}^{2}-0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}12yz\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the double integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}12yz\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{2y}}12yz\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}[6y{z}^{2}]_{z=1}^{z=\\sqrt{2y}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}6y((\\sqrt{2y})^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}6y(2y-1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}12{y}^{2}-6y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[4{y}^{3}-3{y}^{2}]}_{y=1}^{y=2} \\\\  & =4(8-1)-3(4-1) \\\\  & =19\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186134268 1071 "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}2y\\,dV\n$$\n\nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,0\\le x\\le z\\sqrt{3}\\},\n$$\n\nand $D$ is a finite region in $yz$-plane enclosed between the $y$-axes, the line $y=2$ and the line $z=y$ in the first quadrant." 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186134268 1071 "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}2y\\,dV\n$$\n\nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,0\\le x\\le z\\sqrt{3}},\n$$\n\nand $D$ is a finite region in $yz$-plane enclosed between the $y$-axes, the line $y=2$ and the line $z=y$ in the first quadrant." 13194139535791 17592186045417 false] [17592186134285 1071 "Evaluate the mixed integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{y}12z\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ in the $yz$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,0\\le z\\le 4-{y}^{2}\\}.\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186134285 1071 "Evaluate the mixed integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{y}12z\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ in the $yz$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,0\\le z\\le 4-{y}^{2}}.\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 false] [17592186134287 1071 "Evaluate the mixed integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{x}2xyz\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}A\n$$\n\ngiven that the region $D$ in the $xz$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\sqrt{4-2z},\\,0\\le z\\le 2\\}.\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186134287 1071 "Evaluate the mixed integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{x}2xyz\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}A\n$$\n\ngiven that the region $D$ in the $xz$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\sqrt{4-2z},\\,0\\le z\\le 2}.\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 false] [17592186134293 1071 "$$\n{∭}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{∗}^{∗}{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{∗}^{∗}f(x,y,z)\\,dy\\,dz\\,dx\n$$\n\nThe triple integral above is defined over the type III region given by\n\n$$\nS=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,-1\\le z\\le 2x+1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1+x+z\\}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $a+b?$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186134293 1071 "$$\n{∭}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{∗}^{∗}{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{∗}^{∗}f(x,y,z)\\,dy\\,dz\\,dx\n$$\n\nThe triple integral above is defined over the type III region given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,-1\\le z\\le 2x+1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1+x+z}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $a+b?$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 false] [17592186134293 1074 "A type III region in three-dimensional space can be written as\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,{u}_{1}(x,z)\\le y\\le {u}_{2}(x,z)\\}.\n$$\n\nA typical three-dimensional type III region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/06/0603bed12c6af74e46544d8bebc52362aed95a9a5ad7418ea4faaf228f3b5241.png)\n\n \n\nIf $R$ is a type III region, we can express the triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over $R$ as a mixed integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,z)}^{{u}_{2}(x,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y]\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x,z)=0,\\,{u}_{2}(x,z)=1+x+z.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our triple integral can be written as\n\n$$\n{∭}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{1+x+z}f(x,y,z)\\,dy]dA.\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,-1\\le z\\le 2x+1\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by expressing the double integral over $D$ as a repeated integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{1+x+z}f(x,y,z)\\,dy]dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2x+1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1+x+z}f(x,y,z)\\,dy]dz\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{2x+1}{\\int }_{0}^{1+x+z}f(x,y,z)\\,dy\\,dz\\,dx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b=(-1)+(2x+1)=2x.\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186134293 1074 "A type III region in three-dimensional space can be written as\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,{u}_{1}(x,z)\\le y\\le {u}_{2}(x,z)}.\n$$\n\nA typical three-dimensional type III region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/06/0603bed12c6af74e46544d8bebc52362aed95a9a5ad7418ea4faaf228f3b5241.png)\n\n \n\nIf $R$ is a type III region, we can express the triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over $R$ as a mixed integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,z)}^{{u}_{2}(x,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y]\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x,z)=0,\\,{u}_{2}(x,z)=1+x+z.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our triple integral can be written as\n\n$$\n{∭}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{1+x+z}f(x,y,z)\\,dy]dA.\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,-1\\le z\\le 2x+1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by expressing the double integral over $D$ as a repeated integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{1+x+z}f(x,y,z)\\,dy]dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2x+1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1+x+z}f(x,y,z)\\,dy]dz\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{2x+1}{\\int }_{0}^{1+x+z}f(x,y,z)\\,dy\\,dz\\,dx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b=(-1)+(2x+1)=2x.\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 false] [17592186135122 1064 "Until now, we've been dealing with line integrals with respect to arc length. In this lesson, we will learn about other types of line integral. \n\nSuppose that $C$ is a path in the $xy$-plane with initial point $A$ and terminal point $B.$ The line integral of the function $f(x,y)$ **with respect to $\\boldsymbol x$** along the path $C$ is denoted\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nTo understand what this integral represents, we imagine traversing $C$ from $A$ to $B.$ At each point $P$ that lies on $C,$ we project the point on $f$ that lies directly above $P$ onto the plane $y=0.$ Doing this for every point on $C$ traces out a curve. The line integral of $f$ with respect to $x$ gives the (signed) area bounded by this curve and the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/62/62c07a36cbf79459021ee7be125536a8f9c1bc3c193a99eabfcd3fc851b8eb0c.png)\n\nTo calculate a line integral with respect to $x,$ we must first parameterize $C$ using a parameter $t\\in [a,b].$ Then, we apply the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}x  = \\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\dfrac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's take a look at a concrete example." 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186135122 1064 "Until now, we've been dealing with line integrals with respect to arc length. In this lesson, we will learn about other types of line integral. \n\nSuppose that $C$ is a path in the $xy$-plane with initial point $A$ and terminal point $B.$ The line integral of the function $f(x,y)$ **with respect to $\\boldsymbol x$** along the path $C$ is denoted\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nTo understand what this integral represents, we imagine traversing $C$ from $A$ to $B.$ At each point $P$ that lies on $C,$ we project the point on $f$ that lies directly above $P$ onto the plane $y=0.$ Doing this for every point on $C$ traces out a curve. The line integral of $f$ with respect to $x$ gives the (signed) area bounded by this curve and the $x$-axis.\n\nThis integral represents the total area accumulated when [math]f[/math] is projected on the plane [math]y=0[/math] as [math]C[/math] is traversed from the initial point [math]A[/math] to the terminal point [math]B.[/math] \n\n![](images/62/62c07a36cbf79459021ee7be125536a8f9c1bc3c193a99eabfcd3fc851b8eb0c.png)\n\nTo calculate a line integral with respect to $x,$ we must first parameterize $C$ using a parameter $t\\in [a,b].$ Then, we apply the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}x  = \\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\dfrac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's take a look at a concrete example." 13194139535791 17592186045417 false] [17592186135156 1064 "Let's evaluate the triple integral\n\n![](images/ae/aec90ae0e9463c8e7c9164ed980828389070a8bb4a4ded54e2586f6c5a5ea376.svg)\n\nwhere the region ![](images/08/085f4d77675f3cb7323d94feaa9eaa38f2fecb26c0f8483895f9ddf36b8d319d.svg) is shown below.\n\n![](images/3e