n\nEvaluating this using the usual methods, we get\n\n![](images/3f/3f3fe0404f0c16535a37c7a739dd9f00cdc1b37dcfd076606cd57cd15063aabc.svg)\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n![](images/07/07882d044aec2af88f6cc3c3282eadae112f1080bd8d5dc23667a7dcafcf3e1e.svg)" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186135156 1064 "Let's evaluate the triple integral\n\n![](images/ae/aec90ae0e9463c8e7c9164ed980828389070a8bb4a4ded54e2586f6c5a5ea376.svg)\n\nwhere the region ![](images/08/085f4d77675f3cb7323d94feaa9eaa38f2fecb26c0f8483895f9ddf36b8d319d.svg) is shown below.\n\n![](images/3e/3ee0df491365ac117eef84055f9d14aff9e7e5073b69e8e48b0a8bb93bb2f7ee.png)\n\nThe region ![](images/08/085f4d77675f3cb7323d94feaa9eaa38f2fecb26c0f8483895f9ddf36b8d319d.svg) is a type II region. So, our first task is to fully describe this region.\n\nWe start by drawing an arrow parallel to the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-axis that \n\n- enters the region through the *back* surface ![](images/89/89c97ad93c125fc2b6f61d44fe9a90845fd289329d79d506c857f970d7586540.svg) and\n\n- leaves the region through the *front* surface ![](images/7b/7b823cb4664e2fe98090b6a778eba3030abf589930d2526ef5a50fb0aa792b5f.svg)\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/e6/e63827168c78ff5d13898bc436e02f99d834fbd2a10ce43aa98f47001e35044d.png)\n\nSo, our solid can be written as the type II region\n\n![](images/c4/c4a007ae116844babbe81721267c0526be0ee44b8b8007a799031eae12485a59.svg)\n\nwhere\n\n![](images/12/12d667e2c74a83bd8f7fe58be763862fc793d22774695995b56ccda713308381.svg)\n\nis the projection of ![](images/08/085f4d77675f3cb7323d94feaa9eaa38f2fecb26c0f8483895f9ddf36b8d319d.svg) onto the ![](images/3f/3f84f2f8778b7916a5ee013c8a92440e5cbd510ba2768435c3139b45806d2f90.svg)-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/ea/ea267107ec908087a99eaea952caabb0d1a08488657b2c0f70a2502bbf4b869b.png)\n\nTo evaluate our triple integral, we start by expressing it as a mixed integral:\n\n![](images/13/13957704101661a7e52fa89ed897918c142fd1725742cf7b082d72eb54672e66.svg)\n\nNow, since the region ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) is a type I plane region, we can express the region ![](images/08/085f4d77675f3cb7323d94feaa9eaa38f2fecb26c0f8483895f9ddf36b8d319d.svg) as\n\n![](images/53/535700690396dd4444e6f79015a447900af741cf14001fc2c552224e597a948b.svg)\n\nand therefore, our mixed integral can be written as the following repeated integral:\n\n![](images/68/6843abfc93fe5ae9a8f91fd2aaae7c725722ea8349021fb87430ddee5a51b9e2.svg)\n\nEvaluating this using the usual methods, we get\n\n![](images/3f/3f3fe0404f0c16535a37c7a739dd9f00cdc1b37dcfd076606cd57cd15063aabc.svg)\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n![](images/07/07882d044aec2af88f6cc3c3282eadae112f1080bd8d5dc23667a7dcafcf3e1e.svg)" 13194139535791 17592186045417 false] [17592186135158 1064 "Let's evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R 2xz  \\: \\mathrm{d}V\n$$\n\nwhere the region $R$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/9b/9b00702b941e40061ff242e63c0d1b33642cce9aefc1ffa9f88e9d94cd02897a.png)\n\n \n\nThe region $R$ is a type III region. So, our first task is to describe this region fully.\n\nWe start by drawing an arrow parallel to the $y$-axis that \n\n- enters the region through the *left* surface $y=0,$ and\n\n- leave the region through the *right* surface $y=6-x-z,$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/51/5102f786fa91eb862fd3f5ef8f0b2cbc85fbdff6fc616f86f498aa0da8088f14.png)\n\n \n\nSo, our solid can be written as the type III region\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: (x,z) \\in D, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 6-x-z \\big\\},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,z) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 3, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $xz$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/30/30f320f2e77c95c13e59bc3fdd6b273d1ad367018af3cd12bd6d2fde7cc09132.png)\n\nTo evaluate our triple integral, we start by expressing it as a mixed integral:\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_R 2xz \\:\\textrm{d}V\n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\left[ \\int_{0}^{6-x-z} 2xz \\:\\textrm{d}y \\right] \\textrm{d}A\n$$\n\nNow, since the region $D$ is a type I *plane* region, we can express the region $R$ as\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 3, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 6-x-z \\big\\},\n$$\n\nand therefore, our mixed integral can be written as the following repeating integral:\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_D \\left[ \\int_0^{6-x-z} 2xz \\:\\textrm{d}y \\right] \\textrm{d}A\n=\n\\int_{0}^{3} \\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{0}^{6-x-z} 2xz \\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nEvaluating this using the usual methods, we get\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{3} \\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{0}^{6-x-z} 2xz \\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}x = 15.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_R 2xz \\:\\textrm{d}V = 15.\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186135158 1064 "Let's evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R 2xz  \\: \\mathrm{d}V\n$$\n\nwhere the region $R$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/9b/9b00702b941e40061ff242e63c0d1b33642cce9aefc1ffa9f88e9d94cd02897a.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a type III region. So, our first task is to describe this region fully.\n\nWe start by drawing an arrow parallel to the $y$-axis that \n\n- enters the region through the *left* surface $y=0,$ and\n\n- leave the region through the *right* surface $y=6-x-z,$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/51/5102f786fa91eb862fd3f5ef8f0b2cbc85fbdff6fc616f86f498aa0da8088f14.png)\n\nSo, our solid can be written as the type III region\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: (x,z) \\in D, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 6-x-z \\big\\},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,z) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 3, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $xz$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/30/30f320f2e77c95c13e59bc3fdd6b273d1ad367018af3cd12bd6d2fde7cc09132.png)\n\nTo evaluate our triple integral, we start by expressing it as a mixed integral:\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_R 2xz \\:\\textrm{d}V\n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\left[ \\int_{0}^{6-x-z} 2xz \\:\\textrm{d}y \\right] \\textrm{d}A\n$$\n\nNow, since the region $D$ is a type I *plane* region, we can express the region $R$ as\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 3, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 6-x-z \\big\\},\n$$\n\nand therefore, our mixed integral can be written as the following repeating integral:\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_D \\left[ \\int_0^{6-x-z} 2xz \\:\\textrm{d}y \\right] \\textrm{d}A\n=\n\\int_{0}^{3} \\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{0}^{6-x-z} 2xz \\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nEvaluating this using the usual methods, we get\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{3} \\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{0}^{6-x-z} 2xz \\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}x = 15.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_R 2xz \\:\\textrm{d}V = 15.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to [math]y[/math], treating [math]x[/math] and [math]z[/math] as constants: \n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\int_{0}^{3} \\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{0}^{6-x-z} 2xz \\: \\mathrm{d}y \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\: \\mathrm{d}x\n\n&= \\int_{0}^{3} \\int_{0}^{1} \\left[ \\int_{0}^{6-x-z} 2xz \\: \\mathrm{d}y \\right] \\mathrm{d}z \\: \\mathrm{d}x \\\\[5pt]\n\n&= \\int_{0}^{3} \\int_{0}^{1} 2xz \\big[ y \\big]_{y=0}^{y=6-x-z} \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\: \\mathrm{d}x \\\\[5pt]\n\n&= \\int_{0}^{3} \\int_{0}^{1} 2xz(6-x-z) \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\: \\mathrm{d}x \\\\[5pt]\n\n&= \\int_{0}^{3} \\int_{0}^{1} (12xz-2x^2z-2xz^2) \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\: \\mathrm{d}x \n\\end{align*}\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to [math]z[/math], treating [math]x[/math] as a constant: \n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\int_{0}^{3} \\int_{0}^{1} (12xz-2x^2z-2xz^2) \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\: \\mathrm{d}x\n\n&= \\int_{0}^{3} \\left[ \\int_{0}^{1} (12xz-2x^2z-2xz^2) \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\right] \\mathrm{d}x \\\\[5pt]\n\n&= \\int_{0}^{3} \\left[6xz^2-x^2z^2-\\dfrac23xz^3 \\right]_{z=0}^{z=1} \\: \\mathrm{d}x \\\\[5pt]\n\n&= \\int_{0}^{3} \\left( \\dfrac{16}3x-x^2 \\right) \\: \\mathrm{d}x \n\\end{align*}\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to [math]x{:}[/math]\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\int_{0}^{3} \\left( \\dfrac{16}3x-x^2 \\right) \\: \\mathrm{d}x\n\n&= \\left[ \\dfrac{8}3x^2-\\dfrac13x^3 \\right]_{x=0}^{x=3} \\\\[5pt]\n\n&= \\left( \\dfrac83 \\cdot 3^2 - \\dfrac13 \\cdot 3^3 \\right) - 0 \\\\[5pt]\n\n&= 15.\n\\end{align*}" 13194139535791 17592186045417 false] [17592186135159 1064 "When writing down a triple integral as a repeated integral, we can often skip the intermediate step of expressing our triple integral as a mixed integral.\n\nFor example, suppose we wish to evaluate\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R f(x,y,z) \\:\\mathrm{d}V\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the type III region given by \n\n$$\nR= \\big\\{(x,y,z) \\: : \\: {\\color{red}a} \\leq x \\leq {\\color{red}b}, \\: {\\color{blue}v_1(x)} \\leq z \\leq {\\color{blue}v_2(x)}, \\: {\\color{purple}u_1(x,z)} \\leq y \\leq {\\color{purple}u_2(x,z)} \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the projection of $R$ onto the $xz$-plane is a type II plane region. Therefore, we can immediately express our triple integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_R f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}V\n=\n\\int_{\\color{red}a}^{\\color{red}b} \\int_{\\color{blue}v_1(x)}^{\\color{blue}v_2(x)} \\int_{\\color{purple}u_1(x,z)}^{\\color{purple}u_2(x,z)} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186135159 1064 "When writing down a triple integral as a repeated integral, we can often skip the intermediate step of expressing our triple integral as a mixed integral.\n\nFor example, suppose we wish to evaluate\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R f(x,y,z) \\:\\mathrm{d}V\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the type III region given by \n\n$$\nR= \\big\\{(x,y,z) \\: : \\: {\\color{red}a} \\leq x \\leq {\\color{red}b}, \\: {\\color{blue}v_1(x)} \\leq z \\leq {\\color{blue}v_2(x)}, \\: {\\color{purple}u_1(x,z)} \\leq y \\leq {\\color{purple}u_2(x,z)} \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the projection of $R$ onto the $xz$-plane is a type II plane region. Therefore, we can immediately express our triple integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_R f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}V\n=\n\\int_{\\color{red}a}^{\\color{red}b} \\int_{\\color{blue}v_1(x)}^{\\color{blue}v_2(x)} \\int_{\\color{purple}u_1(x,z)}^{\\color{purple}u_2(x,z)} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nHowever, in many cases, the evaluation will be easier if we can decompose the triple integral into simple integrals. \nIn our example above, we can write our triple integral as a repeated integral right away (skipping the step with the double integral):\n\n\\[\n\\displaystyle \\iiint \\limits_{R} 4y \\: \\textrm{d}V \n= \n\\int_{\\color{red}0}^{\\color{red}1} \\int_{\\color{blue}0}^{\\color{blue}x} \\int_{\\color{violet}0}^{\\color{violet}\\sqrt x} 4y \\: \\mathrm{d}y \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\: \\mathrm{d}x\n\\]\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to [math]y[/math], treating [math]x[/math] and [math]z[/math] as constants: \n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{0}^{x} \\int_{0}^{\\sqrt x} 4y \\: \\mathrm{d}y \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\: \\mathrm{d}x\n\n&= \\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{0}^{x} 2\\left[ \\int_{0}^{\\sqrt x} 2y \\: \\mathrm{d}y \\right] \\mathrm{d}z \\: \\mathrm{d}x \\\\[5pt]\n\n&= \\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{0}^{x}  2 \\left[ y^2\\right]_{y=0}^{y=\\sqrt x} \\mathrm{d}z \\: \\mathrm{d}x \\\\[5pt]\n\n&= \\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{0}^{x} 2(({\\sqrt x})^2-0^2 ) \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\: \\mathrm{d}x \\\\[5pt]\n\n&= \\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{0}^{x} 2x \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\: \\mathrm{d}x \n\\end{align*}\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to [math]z[/math], treating [math]x[/math] as a constant: \n\n\\begin{align*}\n \\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{0}^{x} 2x \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\: \\mathrm{d}x \n\n&= \\int_{0}^{1} \\left[ \\int_{0}^{x}2x \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\right] \\mathrm{d}x  \\\\[5pt]\n\n&= \\int_{0}^{1} 2x\\big