joint probability by the corresponding column total, we obtain the conditional probability mass function of $X$ given that $Y=y:$\n\n| ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $\\frac{2}{9}$ | $\\frac{4}{11}$ |\n| $X=3$ | $\\frac{4}{9}$ | $\\frac{5}{11}$ |\n| $X=9$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{2}{11}$ |\n|  | $1$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=0] & =[{\\sum }_{x}{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,0)]-{\\mu }_{X|0}^{2} \\\\  & =[{0}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{9}+{3}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{4}{9}+{9}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}]-{(\\frac{13}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =0+4+27-\\frac{169}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{110}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186189401 1064 "Suppose that $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables, where the conditional expected value of $X$ given $Y=y$ is given by $\\mu_{X \\mid y}$. In other words,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}\\begin{bmatrix}X\\, & \\,Y=y\\end{bmatrix} = \\mu_{X \\mid y}\n$$\n\nThe **conditional variance of $X$ given $Y=y$** is defined as\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}\\begin{bmatrix}X\\, & \\,Y=y\\end{bmatrix} = \\textrm{E}\\big\\begin{bmatrix}(X - \\mu_{X \\mid y} \\big)^2 \\, & \\, Y=y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis is similar to the definition of $\\textrm{Var}[X]$ in that it represents the average (squared) distance of the random variable $X$ from the mean. The difference here is that the expected values are now dependent on the outcome of $Y$.\n\nSimilarly to the case of $\\textrm{Var}[X]$, this often isn't the easiest formula to use in practice. However, it can be shown that the definition of conditional variance is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar\\begin{bmatrix}X & Y = y\\end{bmatrix} &= E\\begin{bmatrix}X^{2} & Y = y\\end{bmatrix} - μ_{X\\begin{vmatrix}y}^{2} \\\\ = [∑_(x)x^{2}f_{X\\mid Y}(x \\mid y)] - μ_{X\\end{vmatrix}y}^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f_{X\\mid Y}(x \\, \\mid \\, y)$ is the conditional PMF of $X$ given $Y$. Note that these formulas are analogous to those for $\\textrm{Var}[X]$.\n\nFinally, we have an analogous definition for the conditional variance of $Y$ given $X=x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar\\begin{bmatrix}Y & X = x\\end{bmatrix} &= E\\begin{bmatrix}(Y - μ_{Y \\mid x})^{2} & X = x\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= E\\begin{bmatrix}Y^{2} & X = x\\end{bmatrix} - μ_{Y\\begin{vmatrix}x}^{2} \\\\ = [∑_(y)y^{2}f_{Y\\mid X}(y \\mid x)] - μ_{Y\\end{vmatrix}x}^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 false] [17592186189401 1064 "Suppose that $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables, where the conditional expected value of $X$ given $Y=y$ is given by $\\mu_{X|y}.$ In other words,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=y] = \\mu_{X|y}.\n$$\n\nThe **conditional variance of $X$ given $Y=y$** is defined as\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=y] = \\textrm{E}\\big[(X - \\mu_{X|y} \\big)^2 \\,|\\, Y=y].\n$$\n\nThis is similar to the definition of $\\textrm{Var}[X]$ in that it represents the average  (squared) distance of the random variable $X$ from the mean. The difference here is that the expected values are now dependent on the outcome of $Y.$\n\nSimilarly to the case of $\\textrm{Var}[X],$ this often isn't the easiest formula to use in practice. However, it can be shown that the definition of conditional variance is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=y] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2} \\\\  & =[{\\sum }_{x}{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f_{X|Y}(x \\,|\\, y)$ is the conditional PMF of $X$ given $Y.$ Note that these formulas are analogous to those for $\\textrm{Var}[X].$\n\nFinally, we have an analogous definition for the conditional variance of $Y$ given $X=x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=x] & =\\text{E}[(Y-{\\mu }_{Y|x})^{2}\\,|\\,X=x] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}\\,|\\,X=x]-{\\mu }_{Y|x}^{2} \\\\  & =[{\\sum }_{y}{y}^{2}\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)]-{\\mu }_{Y|x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417