94139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186198995 1070 17592186045487 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186198995 1071 "Find the projection of the intersection between the ellipsoid $x^{2} + ((y + 1)^{2})/(3) + (z - 1)^{2} = 1$ and the plane $2x + z = 1$ onto the $xy$-plane." 13194139535791 17592186045417 false] [17592186198995 1071 "Find the projection of the intersection between the ellipsoid ${x}^{2}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{3}+(z-1)^{2}=1$ and the plane $2x+z=1$ onto the $xy$-plane." 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186198995 1074 "Since we want to find the projection onto the $xy$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $z.$ First, we solve the equation of the plane for $z$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+z & =1\\,⟹\\,z=1-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $z$ into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{3}+((1-2x)-1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{3}+(-2x)^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{3}+4{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{3} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{1/5}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{3} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1/5}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{3}=1.$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186199015 1070 17592186045487 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186199015 1071 "Find the points of the intersection of the elliptic cone $x^{2} + 2y^{2} = z^{2}$ and the line $x = \\frac{y}{2} = z + 4$." 13194139535791 17592186045417 false] [17592186199015 1071 "Find the points of the intersection of the elliptic cone ${x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ and the line $x=\\frac{y}{2}=z+4.$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186199015 1074 "First, we write the equation of our line in the parametric form:\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{y}{2}=z+4=t\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=t \\\\ y=2t \\\\ z=t-4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSubstituting the above expressions for $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ into the equation of the elliptic cone, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {t}^{2}+2(2t)^{2} & =(t-4)^{2} \\\\ {t}^{2}+8{t}^{2} & ={t}^{2}-8t+16 \\\\ 8{t}^{2}+8t-16 & =0 \\\\ 8({t}^{2}+t-2) & =0 \\\\ 8(t+2)(t-1) & =0 \\\\ t & =-2,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ y=2(-2)=-4 \\\\ z=-2-4=-6,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(-2,-4,-6).$\n\n- For $t=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ y=2(1)=2 \\\\ z=1-4=-3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(1,2,-3).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(-2,-4,-6)$ and $(1,2,-3).$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186199499 1064 "Now, let's consider the intersection of the elliptic paraboloid \n$z=x^2+y^2$\nand the plane \n$z = x+y-\\dfrac{1}{4},$\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/1d/1d4b8db0985d437807f6e580bc6c2c16cacca1ea7c8e49ed4653717cdb3c9e27.png)\n\n \n\nNote that the intersection of the paraboloid and plane is given by the solution to the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}z &={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ z &=x+y-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\n- To find the projection of the intersection onto the $xy$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $z$ from the system. The resulting projection curve will be an ellipse, as depicted below.\n\n![](images/52/520b57cf872ede31d4397d59a833c565147d82a45be1d6b2634f82e1effdff8c.png)\n\n \n\n- To find the projection of the intersection onto the $xz$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $y$ from the system. The resulting projection curve will be an ellipse, as depicted below.\n\n![](images/05/059acf1ec5dadaa98b2cd4d4b6c79884e4facc96dd49664abbc247cfba86aaeb.png)\n\n \n\n- To find the projection of the intersection onto the $yz$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $x$ from the system. The resulting projection curve will be an ellipse, as depicted below.\n\n![](images/80/80d3042d21d2e7265a40739ae1531ac30b03fea16fa67609a4e1b0eac8559411.png)\n\n \n\nLet's see some examples." 13194139535791 17592186045417 false] [17592186199499 1064 "Now, let's consider the intersection of the elliptic paraboloid \n$z=x^2+y^2$\nand the plane \n$z = x+y-\\dfrac{1}{4},$\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/1d/1d4b8db0985d437807f6e580bc6c2c16cacca1ea7c8e49ed4653717cdb3c9e27.png)\n\n \n\nNote that the intersection of the paraboloid and plane is given by the solution to the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ z=x+y-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n- To find the projection of the intersection onto the $xy$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $z$ from the system. The resulting projection curve will be an ellipse, as depicted below.\n\n![](images/52/520b57cf872ede31d4397d59a833c565147d82a45be1d6b2634f82e1effdff8c.png)\n\n \n\n- To find the projection of the intersection onto the $xz$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $y$ from the system. The resulting projection curve will be an ellipse, as depicted below.\n\n![](images/05/059acf1ec5dadaa98b2cd4d4b6c79884e4facc96dd49664abbc247cfba86aaeb.png)\n\n \n\n- To find the projection of the intersection onto the $yz$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $x$ from the system. The resulting projection curve will be an ellipse, as depicted below.\n\n![](images/80/80d3042d21d2e7265a40739ae1531ac30b03fea16fa67609a4e1b0eac8559411.png)\n\n \n\nLet's see some examples." 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186199500 1064 "Suppose we wish to find the points of intersection of the elliptic paraboloid \n$z=x^2+y^2$\nand the line with the equation\n$x = -y = -z+1.$\nThe line, paraboloid, and their points of intersection are shown below.\n\n![](images/51/5159ac99a1bc9f73c01df3c2cc2e6fb2093a3d68e00731609d7b62a6fa55149d.png)\n\n \n\nThe trick is to express the equation of the line in parametric form, substitute the parametric equations into the equation of the surface, and solve for the parameter.\n\nSo, first, we write the equation of our line in the parametric form:\n\n![](images/01/01357a5c6b72d7c976765aeed04db8afa3e3a80e13095d9be1ea2f2594ae5bf5.svg)\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nOur intersection points must belong to the line and the surface simultaneously. So, substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ (-t+1) & =(t)^{2}+(-t)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying this equation, we get\n\n$$\n2t^2+t - 1=0.\n$$\n\nThe solutions to this equation are\n$t=\\dfrac{1}{2}$ and $t=-1.$ \n\nSubstituting these values back into the parametric equations of the line\n\n- For $t=\\dfrac{1}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y &=-(\\frac{1}{2})=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ z &=-(\\frac{1}{2})+1=\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(\\dfrac{1}{2},-\\dfrac{1}{2},\\dfrac{1}{2}).$\n\n- For $t=-1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=(-1)=-1 \\\\ y &=-(-1)=1 \\\\ z &=-(-1)+1=2,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(-1,1,2).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(\\dfrac{1}{2},-\\dfrac{1}{2},\\dfrac{1}{2})$ and $(-1,1,2).$ \n\nLet's see another example." 13194139535791 17592186045417 false] [17592186199500 1064 "Suppose we wish to find the points of intersection of the elliptic paraboloid \n$z=x^2+y^2$\nand the line with the equation\n$x = -y = -z+1.$\nThe line, paraboloid, and their points of intersection are shown below.\n\n![](images/51/5159ac99a1bc9f73c01df3c2cc2e6fb2093a3d68e00731609d7b62a6fa55149d.png)\n\n \n\nThe trick is to express the equation of the line in parametric form, substitute the parametric equations into the equation of the surface, and solve for the parameter.\n\nSo, first, we write the equation of our line in the parametric form:\n\n![](images/01/01357a5c6b72d7c976765aeed04db8afa3e3a80e13095d9be1ea2f2594ae5bf5.svg)\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nOur intersection points must belong to the line and the surface simultaneously. So, substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ (-t+1) & =(t)^{2}+(-t)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying this equation, we get\n\n$$\n2t^2+t - 1=0.\n$$\n\nThe solutions to this equation are\n$t=\\dfrac{1}{2}$ and $t=-1.$ \n\nSubstituting these values back into the parametric equations of the line\n\n- For $t=\\dfrac{1}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y=-(\\frac{1}{2})=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ z=-(\\frac{1}{2})+1=\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(\\dfrac{1}{2},-\\dfrac{1}{2},\\dfrac{1}{2}).$\n\n- For $t=-1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=(-1)=-1 \\\\ y=-(-1)=1 \\\\ z=-(-1)+1=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(-1,1,2).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(\\dfrac{1}{2},-\\dfrac{1}{2},\\dfrac{1}{2})$ and $(-1,1,2).$ \n\nLet's see another example." 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186208843 1038 17592186527975 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186233443 1068 17592186527902 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186234032 1068 17592186527807 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186234373 1068 17592186527858 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186278391 1074 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u+v,\\,u,\\,v⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v),\\