17592186527906 1076 17592186045493 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527906 1077 "selection" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527906 1078 17592186527398 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527906 1079 #uuid "faa2ee71-4e17-514a-a333-0d36f72cde02" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527907 1075 17592186527358 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527907 1076 17592186045492 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527907 1077 "field-1" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527907 1078 17592186527358 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527907 1079 #uuid "fc7e3261-08d2-5e99-8825-f84a4ffcfbd5" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527908 1075 17592186527511 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527908 1076 17592186045492 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527908 1077 "field-1" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527908 1078 17592186527511 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527908 1079 #uuid "fef39881-b36f-5f93-b608-86d132bcd59d" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527909 1075 17592186527761 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527909 1076 17592186045492 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527909 1077 "field-2" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527909 1078 17592186527761 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527909 1079 #uuid "ff55a139-4c0c-56e8-a3f1-ea5a3e41bb14" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527910 1068 17592186527821 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527910 1069 "q-161558" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527910 1070 17592186045487 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527910 1071 "![](images/57/5780b9e67272fdd78a24aab108fb9c672ee76ff2d62376991cea03c7efaa81fe.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral \n${∭}_{R}\\frac{z}{12}\\,dV$\nover the region $R,$ shown above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the front one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527910 1072 #uuid "00568ac8-b77a-5f52-9484-f19c655aa6d0" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527910 1074 "![](images/24/240cc6d74e266579aa848be820fd0014672aa0654f079c079472a896ad44c5fe.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type II region\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 4-y+2z\\},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 4,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 3\\}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/27/27862dcc3effe5801c812f5c8944c0134d13bb38f99d337b0a4e20e9ff815639.png)\n\nHence, we can express the region $R$ as\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 4,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 3,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 4-y+2z\\}\n$$\n\nAs a result, by writing down our triple integral as a repeated integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\frac{z}{12}\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{4-y+2z}\\frac{z}{12}\\,dx\\,dz\\,dy.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $x$, treating $y$ and $z$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{4-y+2z}\\frac{z}{12}\\,dx\\,dz\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{3}[{\\int }_{0}^{4-y+2z}\\frac{z}{12}\\,dx]dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{z}{12}[x]_{x=0}^{x=4-y+2z}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{z}{12}(4-y+2z)\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{12}(4z-yz+2{z}^{2})\\,dz\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z$, treating $y$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{12}(4z-yz+2{z}^{2})\\,dz\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}[{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{12}(4z-yz+2{z}^{2})\\,dz]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{1}{12}{[2{z}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}y{z}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{z}^{3}]}_{z=0}^{z=3}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{1}{12}(18-\\frac{9}{2}y+18)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}(3-\\frac{3}{8}y)\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}(3-\\frac{3}{8}y)\\,dy & ={[3y-\\frac{3}{16}{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=4} \\\\  & =(12-3)-0 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527911 1068 17592186527805 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527911 1069 "q-161666" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527911 1070 17592186045487 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527911 1071 "Evaluate the mixed integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{x}2x\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}A\n$$\n\ngiven that the region $D$ in the $xz$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le z\\le 2-x\\}.\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527911 1072 #uuid "0597a6d4-1752-5cad-a458-a8b2a80e68a1" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527911 1074 "First, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $y,$ treating $x$ and $z$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{x}2x\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}2x[y]_{y=0}^{y=x}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type I region in the $xz$-plane.\nTherefore, to evaluate our double integral as a repeated integral over this region, we integrate with respect to $z$ and then with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2-x}2{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2{x}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2-x}\\,\\text{d}z]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2{x}^{2}{[z]}_{z=0}^{z=2-x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2{x}^{2}(2-x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(4{x}^{2}-2{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{4}{3}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{4}]}_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{32}{3}-8)-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527912 1068 17592186527853 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527912 1069 "q-161568" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527912 1070 17592186045487 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527912 1071 "Evaluate the mixed integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{y}^{y+z}2x\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ in the $yz$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 2,\\,0\\le z\\le y\\}.\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527912 1072 #uuid "0884b1e2-1bba-5f2c-8a6a-f7af258e16fe" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527912 1074 "First, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $x,$ treating $y$ and $z$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{y}^{y+z}2x\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}[{x}^{2}]_{x=y}^{x=y+z}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(y+z)^{2}-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2yz+{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the double integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2yz+{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{y}2yz+{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{[y{z}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{z}^{3}]}_{z=0}^{z=y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{y}^{3}+\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{4}{3}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{4}]}_{y=1}^{y=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(16-1) \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527913 1068 17592186527869 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527913 1069 "q-75897" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527913 1070 17592186045485 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527913 1071 "The point $P$ with coordinates $(-2,0)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x).$ Which point must lie on $y=-f(x)?$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527913 1072 #uuid "0ae90b83-eb2c-51a0-b2e2-8e297e4d91e3" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527913 1074 "The transformation $y=-f(x)$ reflects the curve $y=f(x)$ in the $x$-axis.\n\nFirst, notice that the point $P(-2,0)$ lies on the $x$-axis. So, under the action of the transformation, this point will be reflected to itself.\n\n![](images/80/808bdc6ebdad390ce7bec9f2ce43dacefe8a120c6b86c48c5740e02228867b63.png)\n\nWe conclude that the point  $(-2,0)$  must lie on the graph of $y=-f(x).$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527914 1068 17592186527862 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527914 1069 "q-161764" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527914 1070 17592186045487 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527914 1071 "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}4xy\\,dV\n$$\n\nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,x\\le y\\le \\sqrt{1-z}\\},\n$$\n\nand $D$ is a finite region in $xz$-plane enclosed between the $x$-axis, the $z$-axis, and the curve $z=1-{x}^{2},$ in the first quadrant." 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527914 1072 #uuid "0bcb5cdc-84fc-59d4-a070-2f4375bf7e4a" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527914 1074 "Writing down our triple integral as a mixed integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}4xy\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{x}^{\\sqrt{1-z}}4xy\\,dy]dA.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $y,$ treating $x$ and $z$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{x}^{\\sqrt{1-z}}4xy\\,dy]dA & ={∬}_{D}2x[{y}^{2}]_{y=x}^{y=\\sqrt{1-z}}\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2x(\\,(1-z)-{x}^{2})\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2x(1-z)-2{x}^{3}\\,dA\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now evaluate this double integral over the region $D$ in the $xz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/95/95a2b4e28dbfae5ce447b3b975b2f74bf04883441217f42a24636970948402a0.png)\n\n  \n\nWe can evaluate our double integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2x(1-z)-2{x}^{3}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-z}}2x(1-z)-2{x}^{3}\\,dx\\,dz \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-z)[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-z}}2x\\,dx]dz+{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-z}}-2{x}^{3}\\,dx]dz \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-z)[{x}^{2}]_{x=0}^{x=\\sqrt{1-z}}dz+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=\\sqrt{1-z}}dz \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-z)^{2}\\,dz-\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-z)^{2}\\,dz \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-z)^{2}\\,dz \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{[-(1-z)^{3}]}_{z=0}^{z=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527915 1068 17592186527852 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527915 1069 "q-94780" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527915 1070 17592186045487 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527915 1071 "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the elliptic cone ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2{z}^{2}$ and the line $x=y=z+6.$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527915 1072 #uuid "0c6c3541-8949-51d1-86fa-598cbd047050" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527915 1074 "First, we write the equation of our line in parametric form:\n\n$$\nx=y=z+6=t\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=t \\\\ y=t \\\\ z=t-6,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSubstituting the above expressions for $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ into the equation of the elliptic cone, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2{z}^{2} \\\\ {t}^{2}+{t}^{2} & =2(t-6)^{2} \\\\ {t}^{2}+{t}^{2} & =2({t}^{2}-12t+36) \\\\ 2{t}^{2} & =2{t}^{2}-24t+72 \\\\ 24t & =72 \\\\ t & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute our solution back into the equations of the line. For $t=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=3 \\\\ y=3 \\\\ z=3-6=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $(3,3-3).$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527916 1068 17592186527823 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527916 1069 "q-161932" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527916 1070 17592186045487 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527916 1071 "![](images/6f/6f4bf1fe2f1a49f84b56702c4a83272c2f927ea4774eb677f43e525bf6cfb1f7.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral \n${∭}_{R}8x\\,dV$\nover the region $R,$ shown above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the right one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527916 1072 #uuid "0d6e2493-0d92-5bdf-8874-580184f51513" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527916 1074 "![](images/71/71f3fd0cbbde8adeca71688d6b53e2cd4cdf72076be3efa770cfebb8c10ffb76.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type III region\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 3-\\frac{3x}{4}-\\frac{z}{2}\\},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 3\\}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $xz$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/cd/cd84968e9e5300a18d087ba566202aba2b7166818a590cca03eac90d4f3f7a89.png)\n\nHence, the type III representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 3,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 3-\\frac{3x}{4}-\\frac{z}{2}\\}\n$$\n\nAs a result, by writing down our triple integral as a repeated integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}8x\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3-3x/4-z/2}8x\\,dy\\,dz\\,dx.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $y$, treating $x$ and $z$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3-3x/4-z/2}8x\\,dy\\,dz\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3}[{\\int }_{0}^{3-3x/4-z/2}8x\\,dy]dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3}8x[y]_{y=0}^{y=3-3x/4-z/2}\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3}8x(3-\\frac{3x}{4}-\\frac{z}{2})\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3}(24x-6{x}^{2}-4xz)\\,dz\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z$, treating $x$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3}(24x-6{x}^{2}-4xz)\\,dz\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{3}(24x-6{x}^{2}-4xz)\\,dz]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[24xz-6{x}^{2}z-2x{z}^{2}]}_{z=0}^{z=3}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(72x-18{x}^{2}-18x)\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(54x-18{x}^{2})\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(54x-18{x}^{2})\\,dx & ={[27{x}^{2}-6{x}^{3}]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =(27-6)-0 \\\\  & =21.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527917 1068 17592186527884 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527917 1069 "q-75282" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527917 1070 17592186045487 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527917 1071 "Find the equation of the tangent line to the polar curve $r=1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ at $\\theta =\\pi ,$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,{x}^{′}(\\theta )=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527917 1072 #uuid "0e54ebb5-b13c-5b43-a434-8a77b6f9b884" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527917 1074 "To find the equation of the tangent line to a polar curve at a point $P,$ we follow four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Calculate the derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}:$\n\nIn this case, we're given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,{x}^{′}(\\theta )=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the slope of the tangent at $P:$\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )}{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1-2\\cdot 0}{-0-2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P:$\n\nWe compute the $xy$-coordinates of $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =(1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =(1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi  \\\\  & =(1-0)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ {y}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =(1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =(1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi  \\\\  & =(1-0)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(-1,0).$\n\n**Step 4**: Using point-slope form, find the equation of the tangent:\n\nWe find the equation of the tangent as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (x-(-1)) \\\\ y-0 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (x+1) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/70/7065086643b98b95b5d6face85544f33b00f73614a855fb9846ba68e5d987a33.png)" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527918 1068 17592186527842 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527918 1069 "q-161725" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527918 1070 17592186045486 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527918 1071 "$$\n{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{x}^{\\sqrt{1-z}}4xy\\,dy]dA,\n$$\n\nEvaluate the repeated integral above given that $D$ is a region in the $xz$-plane bounded by the lines $z=0,$ $x=0,$ and the curve $z+{x}^{2}=1.$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527918 1072 #uuid "0f4e6580-868c-593b-ba50-63aa6d6e6e42" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527918 1074 "First, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $y,$ treating $x$ and $z$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{x}^{\\sqrt{1-z}}4xy\\,dy]dA & ={∬}_{D}2x[{y}^{2}]_{y=x}^{y=\\sqrt{1-z}}\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2x(1-z-{x}^{2})\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\,2x(1-z)-2{x}^{3}\\,)\\,dA\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to calculate this double integral, we plot $D$ as a type I plane region in the $xz$-plane.\n\n![](images/80/8079d8f9796c1f863a515ced0091c1f420318be1c4bfe0eb2da641bddfa426ce.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, to evaluate our double integral as a repeated integral over this region, we integrate with respect to $z$ and then with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(\\,2x(1-z)-2{x}^{3}\\,)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-{x}^{2}}(\\,2x(1-z)-2{x}^{3}\\,)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x[-(1-z)^{2}]_{z=0}^{z=1-{x}^{2}}-2{x}^{3}[z]_{z=0}^{z=1-{x}^{2}}\\,)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-{x}^{5}+x-2{x}^{3}+2{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{5}+x-2{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{6}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{4}]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527919 1068 17592186527836 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527919 1069 "q-122472" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527919 1070 17592186045487 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527919 1071 "A curve is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=3{t}^{4},\\,y=8t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is vertical." 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527919 1072 #uuid "14b24104-4820-5739-b269-6386c5a0fdfc" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527919 1074 "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is vertical if ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${y}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=12{t}^{3},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=8.\n$$\n\nSolving ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n12{t}^{3}=0\\,⟹\\,t=0.\n$$\n\nNote that ${y}^{′}(0)=8\\ne 0.$ Therefore, we conclude that when $t=0$ the tangent to the curve is vertical." 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527920 1068 17592186527829 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527920 1069 "q-103902" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527920 1070 17592186045487 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527920 1071 "Let $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and let ${P}_{3}(x)$ be the third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=1.$ Use the Lagrange error bound to find an upper bound for the error when ${P}_{3}(x)$ is used to approximate $\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527920 1072 #uuid "15fdecd3-2bb4-5c94-a4a3-305e0c26984d" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527920 1074 "The Lagrange error bound states that an upper bound for the error ${R}_{3}$ obtained when using the third-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{3}$ to approximate $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{M}{4!}|x-1{|}^{4},\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ is the maximum value of $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|$ on the interval between $a=1$ and $x=2.$ \n\nPlugging $x=2$ into the above formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{3}| & \\le \\frac{M}{4!}|2-1{|}^{4} \\\\ |{R}_{3}| & \\le \\frac{M}{24}|1{|}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $M,$ we need to compute $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|$ and then maximize it on the interval $[1,2].$ Taking the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(x) & =-\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}} \\\\ |{f}^{(4)}(x)| & =|-\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}}|=\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|=\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}}$ is a decreasing function in the interval $[1,2],$  its value is greatest at $x=1.$ So\n\n$$\nM=|{f}^{(4)}(1)|=\\frac{6}{(1)^{4}}=6,\n$$\n\nand therefore, the upper bound is\n\n$$\n|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{6}{24}|1{|}^{4}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527921 1068 17592186527905 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527921 1069 "q-28934" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527921 1070 17592186045487 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527921 1071 "$\\operatorname{cos}⁡{45}^{∘}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡{90}^{∘}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡{0}^{∘}=$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527921 1072 #uuid "1c16c3e2-4221-5f39-9385-b4d17a1e4871" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527921 1074 "The angles ${45}^{∘},$ ${90}^{∘}$ and ${0}^{∘}$ are special angles, and their trigonometric ratios have the values\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡{45}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡{90}^{∘}=0,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡{0}^{∘}=1.\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{45}^{∘}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡{90}^{∘}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡{0}^{∘} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+0+1 & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}+2}{2}. & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527922 1068 17592186527880 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527922 1069 "q-122943" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527922 1070 17592186045486 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527922 1071 "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4t\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{t}{\\pi }),\\,y=t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,t\\in (0,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=\\pi $ on the curve. Determine the point at which the normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $x$-axis." 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527922 1072 #uuid "1cf055f3-7724-5017-8bd4-b9d8370b23a5" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527922 1074 "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=\\pi $ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{\\pi })=4\\pi (0)=0, \\\\ y & =\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi =-\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to determine the equation of the normal at the point $(0,-\\pi ).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{t}{\\pi })+4,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{t}{\\pi })+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=\\pi ,$ the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=\\pi } & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi }{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{\\pi })+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{4\\cdot 0+4}=-\\,\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{\\,(-\\frac{1}{4})\\,}=4\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(0,-\\pi )$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=4.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(-\\pi ) & =4(x-0) \\\\ y & =4x-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe normal intersects the $x$-axis when $y=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-\\pi  & =0 \\\\ 4x & =\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},0)$ is the $x$-intercept." 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527923 1068 17592186527834 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527923 1069 "q-352352" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527923 1070 17592186045485 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527923 1071 "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{n}}{n+1}{x}^{n+1}.\n$$\n\nCalculate the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor series expansion about $x=0$ of ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+\\,27{x}^{3}+\\,⋯$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527923 1072 #uuid "2b0cfae1-dde2-5a5f-b5b7-77aa05d10cca" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527923 1074 "Writing the first few terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{n}}{n+1}{x}^{n+1}=x+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+3{x}^{3}+\\frac{27}{4}{x}^{4}+⋯.\n$$\n\nTo find ${f}^{′}(x),$ we differentiate term-by-term. This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=1+3x+9{x}^{2}+27{x}^{3}+⋯.\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527924 1068 17592186527866 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527924 1069 "q-183347" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527924 1070 17592186045485 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527924 1071 "$\\,$ If $x\\in (0,2),$ then $|x|<2.$\n\nWhich of the following is logically equivalent to the conditional predicate above?" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527924 1072 #uuid "2e5452f1-e9dc-544d-b364-2b179ccf8284" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527924 1074 "Let's denote by $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ the following predicates:\n\n- $P(x):$ $x\\in (0,2).$\n\n- $Q(x):$ $|x|<2.$\n\nThen, our initial sentence can be written as $P(x)⇒Q(x),$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n¬P(x)∨Q(x).\n$$\n\nThe negation of $P(x)$ can be written as\n\n- $¬P(x):$ $x\\notin (0,2).$\n\nTherefore, the given sentence is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\,$ $x\\notin (0,2),$ or $|x|<2.$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527925 1068 17592186527817 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527925 1069 "q-124272" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527925 1070 17592186045485 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527925 1071 "![](images/f5/f5f5b235b54d2acd20297ab365d9b93907111b3fec9a86776c28e93fdfe105ee.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y,$ where $C$ is the path along the line segment from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(2,3),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨2t,\\,3t⟩.$*" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527925 1072 #uuid "2f9d1bf2-2ca6-5610-aed1-5f5054aae288" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527925 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a line segment with endpoints $(0,0)$ and $(2,3).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨(0+t(2-0)),\\,(0+t(3-0))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,\\,3t⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t)=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(2t)^{2}+(3t)^{2} \\\\  & =4{t}^{2}+9{t}^{2} \\\\  & =13{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}13{t}^{2}\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =39{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527926 1068 17592186527889 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527926 1069 "q-146225" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527926 1070 17592186045487 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527926 1071 "| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | $Y=8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $X=4$ | $\\frac{5}{16}$ | $\\frac{1}{16}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ |\n\nSuppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Find $\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=2]$ given that $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above and the corresponding conditional expected value is ${\\mu }_{Y|2}=\\frac{11}{2}.$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527926 1072 #uuid "3104fe5f-1fab-5144-bd0b-8c85fa4d01fa" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527926 1074 "Recall that the conditional variance of $Y$ given $X=x$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=x] & =\\text{E}[(Y-{\\mu }_{Y|x})^{2}\\,|\\,X=x] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}\\,|\\,X=x]-{\\mu }_{Y|x}^{2} \\\\  & =[{\\sum }_{y}{y}^{2}\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)]-{\\mu }_{Y|x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order to find ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x),$ let's first find the marginal distribution for $X,$ corresponding to the row totals: \n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | $Y=8$ | ${f}_{X}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $X=4$ | $\\frac{5}{16}$ | $\\frac{1}{16}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n\nThe conditional probability mass function of $Y$ given that $X=x$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, dividing each joint probability by the corresponding row total, we obtain the conditional probability mass function of $Y$ given that $X=x:$\n\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | $Y=8$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ |\n| $X=4$ | $\\frac{5}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=2] & =[{\\sum }_{y}{y}^{2}\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,2)]-{\\mu }_{Y|2}^{2} \\\\  & =[{2}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}+{4}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}+{8}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}]-{(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =1+4+32-\\frac{121}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527927 1068 17592186527891 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527927 1069 "q-146201" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527927 1070 17592186045485 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527927 1071 "$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,2)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}, & x=5 \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}, & x=10 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSuppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Find the conditional variance $\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=2]$ given that the conditional probability mass function ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,2)$ is shown above and the corresponding conditional expected value is ${\\mu }_{X|2}=8.$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527927 1072 #uuid "315997a1-7d2c-5dee-aeca-23b06ca1daaa" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527927 1074 "Recall that the conditional variance of $X$ given $Y=y$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=y] & =\\text{E}[(X-{\\mu }_{X|y})^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2} \\\\  & =[{\\sum }_{x}{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=y] & =[{\\sum }_{x}{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,2)]-{\\mu }_{X|2}^{2} \\\\  & =[(5)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{5}+(10)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{5}]-(8)^{2} \\\\  & =10+60-64 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527928 1068 17592186527832 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527928 1069 "q-163715" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527928 1070 17592186045486 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527928 1071 "![](images/5b/5b7552bbde56a68bfca44adb0501a4a4a8ae0a1105d3dc9039d05bfb6fd405c9.png)\n\nGiven that $R$ is the type III region shown above, \n${∭}_{R}2y\\,\\text{d}V=$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527928 1072 #uuid "3242c4d8-39d7-5463-b665-91b0cfa5917f" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527928 1074 "For a type III region $R$ with projection $D$ onto the $xz$-plane, the triple integral of a continuous function $f(x,y,z)$ over $R$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,z)}^{{u}_{2}(x,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe consider the region $R$ as a type III region enclosed between the back surface $y={u}_{1}(x,z)=1$ and the front surface $y={u}_{2}(x,z)=2.$\n\n![](images/2f/2f1112a1fa4e540ce8ef2d1337383a2adcb378ba519cfb9c49a4521e5bc1b02f.png)\n\nSo, the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,1\\le y\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ as a type II *plane* region in the $xz$-plane with the left curve\n\n$$\nx={z}^{2}.\n$$\n\n![](images/2d/2dcbae12ed3351892c63117f86bab7ffe6e1818b7a16666b42c2efdcc6bd787f.png)\n\nHence, $D=\\{(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,{z}^{2}\\le x\\le 1\\}.$\n\nTherefore, we write the triple integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}2y\\,\\text{d}V & ={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{1}^{2}2y\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{z}^{2}}^{1}[{\\int }_{1}^{2}2y\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{z}^{2}}^{1}[{y}^{2}]_{y=1}^{y=2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{z}^{2}}^{1}3\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527929 1068 17592186527887 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527929 1069 "q-122387" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527929 1070 17592186045487 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527929 1071 "![](images/b8/b8e332d7f53868313cc589e4ce5e991d2bcce88d15613111c4d0f920b678830d.png)\n\nWhich expression gives the total finite area enclosed between the curves $x={y}^{4}$ and $x={y}^{2},$ as shown above?\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527929 1072 #uuid "33a96099-4464-57a6-87f9-11f8046b2b50" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527929 1074 "Let $f(y)={y}^{4}$ and $g(y)={y}^{2}.$ First, we find the intersections of the two curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ {y}^{4} & ={y}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{4}-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}({y}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}(y-1)(y+1) & =0 \\\\ y & =-1,0,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area we want consists of two regions: ${A}_{1}$ and ${A}_{2}.$\n\n![](images/8b/8b4cbf07b9c2a6d949a58f39489889d2a3fdd4690664e530386f6f61d9aac5fd.png)\n\n- For the area ${A}_{1},$ we have $y\\in [-1,0],$ and the right function is $g(y)$ while the left function is $f(y).$ So, the area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(g(y)-f(y))\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}({y}^{2}-{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the area ${A}_{2},$ we have $y\\in [0,1],$ and the right function is $g(y)$ while the left function is $f(y).$ So, the area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(g(y)-f(y))\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{2}-{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}={\\int }_{-1}^{0}({y}^{2}-{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{2}-{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527930 1068 17592186527839 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527930 1069 "q-266630" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527930 1070 17592186045487 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527930 1071 "$6\\operatorname{sec}⁡{60}^{∘}+3\\operatorname{csc}⁡{30}^{∘}+\\operatorname{cot}⁡{30}^{∘}=${{field-1}}" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527930 1072 #uuid "3ac560de-8de4-5482-a34a-4c1b0b94d0a8" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527930 1074 "Remember that $sec$  is the reciprocal of $cos,$  $csc$ is the reciprocal of $sin$  and $cot$  is the reciprocal of $tan.$\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡{60}^{∘}=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{60}^{∘}},\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡{30}^{∘}=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}},\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡{30}^{∘}=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡{30}^{∘}}.\n$$\n\nThe angle ${60}^{∘}$ and ${30}^{∘}$ are special, and its trigonometric ratios have the values\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡{60}^{∘}=\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}=\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡{30}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\operatorname{sec}⁡{60}^{∘}+3\\operatorname{csc}⁡{30}^{∘}+\\operatorname{cot}⁡{30}^{∘} & = \\\\ 6(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{60}^{∘}})+3(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}})+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡{30}^{∘}} & = \\\\ \\frac{6}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{60}^{∘}}+\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}}+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡{30}^{∘}} & = \\\\ \\frac{6}{(\\frac{1}{2})}+\\frac{3}{(\\frac{1}{2})}+\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})} & = \\\\ 12+6+\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}} & = \\\\ 18+\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}} & = \\\\ 18+\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}} & = \\\\ 18+\\sqrt{3}. & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527931 1068 17592186527820 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527931 1069 "q-5987" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527931 1070 17592186045485 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527931 1071 "$\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{60}^{∘}=$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527931 1072 #uuid "3e80bab2-07dc-55db-9807-7a129c6e1f5b" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527931 1074 "The angles ${30}^{∘}$ and ${60}^{∘}$ are special, and their trigonometric ratios have the  values\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡{60}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{60}^{∘} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} & = \\\\ 0 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527932 1068 17592186527876 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527932 1069 "q-146245" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527932 1070 17592186045487 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527932 1071 "| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ | $\\frac{2}{9}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ |\n| $X=3$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ |\n\nSuppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Find $\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=3]$ given that $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above and the corresponding conditional expected value is ${\\mu }_{Y|3}=\\frac{1}{4}.$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527932 1072 #uuid "41ffd91c-a5ef-5456-865a-acafdbd924a3" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527932 1074 "Recall that the conditional variance of $Y$ given $X=x$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=x] & =\\text{E}[(Y-{\\mu }_{Y|x})^{2}\\,|\\,X=x] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}\\,|\\,X=x]-{\\mu }_{Y|x}^{2} \\\\  & =[{\\sum }_{y}{y}^{2}\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)]-{\\mu }_{Y|x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order to find ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x),$ let's first find the marginal distribution for $X,$ corresponding to the row totals: \n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | ${f}_{X}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ | $\\frac{2}{9}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ | $\\frac{2}{9}$ |\n| $X=3$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ | $\\frac{4}{9}$ |\n\nThe conditional probability mass function of $Y$ given that $X=x$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, dividing each joint probability by the corresponding row total, we obtain the conditional probability mass function of $Y$ given that $X=x:$\n\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $1$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ |\n| $X=3$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=3] & =[{\\sum }_{y}{y}^{2}\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,3)]-{\\mu }_{Y|3}^{2} \\\\  & =[{0}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}+{1}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}]-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =0+\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527933 1068 17592186527896 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527933 1069 "q-352556" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527933 1070 17592186045487 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527933 1071 "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-1)^{n+1}\\frac{6}{n(n+2)}{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nFind the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }$\n{{field-1}}$(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{n-1}$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527933 1072 #uuid "4358f8d2-0190-5bf2-b45f-aefdcdef214a" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527933 1074 "Writing the terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=2x-\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2}+⋯+{{(-1)^{n+1}\\frac{6}{n(n+2)}{x}^{n}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above series term-by-term gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=2-\\frac{3}{2}x+⋯+{{(-1)^{n+1}\\frac{6}{n+2}{x}^{n-1}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\n$$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{n+2}(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{n-1}\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527934 1068 17592186527824 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527934 1068 17592186527855 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527934 1069 "q-350382" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527934 1070 17592186045487 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527934 1071 "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t),\\,y={e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t),\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is vertical. Do not round the answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the tangent to the curve is vertical at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527934 1072 #uuid "43c9679e-fdc0-5c6d-a6ac-430a80de179e" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527934 1074 "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is vertical if ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${y}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)),\\,{y}^{′}(t)={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\n$$\n\nSolving ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)} & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡(t)=1 \\\\ t & =\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & ={e}^{\\frac{\\pi }{4}}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})]=\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\frac{\\pi }{4}}\\ne 0, \\\\ {y}^{′}(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}) & ={e}^{\\frac{5\\pi }{4}}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})]=-\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\frac{5\\pi }{4}}\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the tangent to the curve is vertical at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $t=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527935 1068 17592186527822 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527935 1069 "q-161891" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527935 1070 17592186045487 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527935 1071 "![](images/3c/3cba44923f4f583cf6e01ac87cdd6e5ca73a75993eef8322cd01bf44416f84f4.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral \n${∭}_{R}4x\\,dV$\nover the region $R,$ shown above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the right one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527935 1072 #uuid "43d7c496-4713-50a7-87bb-4a90921bae35" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527935 1074 "![](images/f1/f112dd83a8cbb7563979dc170db23db7e178ba1762aca23fd4073c50e1c7a343.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type III region\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 5-x-z\\},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $xz$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/71/71974fddc8e0e0b6a12fc6a248c15eea08c9f6b8a0823d50aede8aadc31a41fa.png)\n\nHence, the type III representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 5-x-z\\}\n$$\n\nAs a result, by writing down our triple integral as a repeated integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}4x\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{5-x-z}4x\\,dy\\,dz\\,dx.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $y$, treating $x$ and $z$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{5-x-z}4x\\,dy\\,dz\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{5-x-z}4x\\,dy]dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}4x[y]_{y=0}^{y=5-x-z}\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}4x(5-x-z)\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(20x-4{x}^{2}-4xz)\\,dz\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z$, treating $x$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(20x-4{x}^{2}-4xz)\\,dz\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}(20x-4{x}^{2}-4xz)\\,dz]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[20xz-4{x}^{2}z-2x{z}^{2}]}_{z=0}^{z=2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(32x-8{x}^{2})\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(32x-8{x}^{2})\\,dx & ={[16{x}^{2}-\\frac{8{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =(64-\\frac{64}{3})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{128}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527936 1068 17592186527898 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527936 1069 "q-118775" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527936 1070 17592186045485 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527936 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527936 1072 #uuid "46e4a876-e7eb-598f-bbec-ff44b3be1391" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527936 1074 "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{2}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{2}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x){|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2a)) \\\\  & =0-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{2}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527937 1068 17592186527800 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527937 1069 "q-146274" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527937 1070 17592186045486 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527937 1071 "| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ | $Y=9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{2}{9}$ | $\\frac{10}{27}$ | $\\frac{5}{27}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\frac{1}{27}$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ | $\\frac{2}{27}$ |\n\nSuppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Find $\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=9]$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above and the corresponding conditional expected value is ${\\mu }_{X|9}=\\frac{9}{7}.$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527937 1072 #uuid "48b554a2-761e-5c5b-ad17-fc83b8df8618" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527937 1074 "Recall that the conditional variance of $X$ given $Y=y$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=y] & =\\text{E}[(X-{\\mu }_{X|y})^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2} \\\\  & =[{\\sum }_{x}{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order to find ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y),$ let's first find the marginal distribution of $Y,$ corresponding to the column totals: \n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ | $Y=9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{2}{9}$ | $\\frac{10}{27}$ | $\\frac{5}{27}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\frac{1}{27}$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ | $\\frac{2}{27}$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $\\frac{7}{27}$ | $\\frac{13}{27}$ | $\\frac{7}{27}$ |\n\nThe conditional probability mass function of $X$ given that $Y=y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, dividing each joint probability by the corresponding column total, we obtain the conditional probability mass function of $X$ given that $Y=y:$\n\n| ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ | $Y=9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{6}{7}$ | $\\frac{10}{13}$ | $\\frac{5}{7}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\frac{1}{7}$ | $\\frac{3}{13}$ | $\\frac{2}{7}$ |\n|  | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=9] & =[{\\sum }_{x}{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,9)]-{\\mu }_{X|9}^{2} \\\\  & =[{1}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{5}{7}+{2}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{7}]-{(\\frac{9}{7})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{7}+\\frac{8}{7}-\\frac{81}{49} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{49}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527938 1068 17592186527903 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527938 1069 "q-94952" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527938 1070 17592186045487 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527938 1071 "Which of the following best describes the projection of the intersection between the elliptic cone ${x}^{2}+2(y-1)^{2}=6(z+3)^{2}$ and the plane $x+y-4=0$ onto the $yz$-plane?" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527938 1072 #uuid "4b5ee114-4f6d-532e-ba84-9309871f16a7" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527938 1074 "Since we want to find the projection onto the $yz$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $x.$ First, we solve the equation of the plane for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx+y-4=0\\,⟹\\,x=4-y\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $x$ into the equation of the elliptic cone, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4-y)^{2}+2(y-1)^{2} & =6(z+3)^{2} \\\\ 16-8y+{y}^{2}+2({y}^{2}-2y+1) & =6(z+3)^{2} \\\\ 16-8y+{y}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-4y+2 & =6(z+3)^{2} \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-12y+18 & =6(z+3)^{2} \\\\ 3({y}^{2}-4y+4)-12+18 & =6(z+3)^{2} \\\\ 3(y-2)^{2}+6 & =6(z+3)^{2} \\\\ 6(z+3)^{2}-3(y-2)^{2} & =6 \\\\ (z+3)^{2}-\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the equation of a hyperbola centered at $(2,-3)$ in the $yz$-plane." 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527939 1068 17592186527877 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527939 1069 "q-11372" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527939 1070 17592186045487 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527939 1071 "Calculate $\\int \\frac{9x-38}{(4-x)^{2}(2-x)}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527939 1072 #uuid "504684ef-507d-57b1-81c3-7c9672d3e28f" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527939 1074 "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{9x-38}{(4-x)^{2}(2-x)}=\\frac{A}{(4-x)}+\\frac{B}{(4-x)^{2}}+\\frac{C}{(2-x)}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(4-x)^{2}(2-x)$ and get\n\n$$\n9x-38=A(4-x)(2-x)+B(2-x)+C(4-x)^{2}.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=4,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $C$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9(4)-38 & =A(4-4)(2-4)+B(2-4)+C(4-4)^{2} \\\\ 36-38 & =B(-2) \\\\ B & =\\frac{-2}{-2} \\\\ B & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=2,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9(2)-38 & =A(4-2)(2-2)+B(2-2)+C(4-2)^{2} \\\\ 18-38 & =C{(2)}^{2} \\\\ C & =\\frac{-20}{4} \\\\ C & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known values of $B$ and $C,$ we can solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9(0)-38 & =A(4-0)(2-0)+B(2-0)+C(4-0)^{2} \\\\ -38 & =8A+2B+16C \\\\ -38 & =8A+2(1)+16(-5) \\\\ -38 & =8A-78 \\\\ 8A & =78-38 \\\\ A & =\\frac{40}{8} \\\\ A & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{9x-38}{(4-x)^{2}(2-x)}=\\frac{5}{(4-x)}+\\frac{1}{(4-x)^{2}}-\\frac{5}{(2-x)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{9x-38}{(4-x)^{2}(2-x)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{5}{(4-x)}+\\frac{1}{(4-x)^{2}}-\\frac{5}{(2-x)})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\int \\frac{1}{4-x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{(4-x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x-5\\int \\frac{1}{2-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|4-x|+\\frac{1}{4-x}+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2-x|+K,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an integral constant." 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527940 1068 17592186527844 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527940 1069 "q-244570" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527940 1070 17592186045487 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527940 1071 "$\\operatorname{sin}⁡{0}^{∘}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡{60}^{∘}=${{field-1}}" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527940 1072 #uuid "5201241f-04a1-5e84-80f6-a8d23279f733" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527940 1074 "The angles of ${0}^{∘},\\,{30}^{∘},$ and ${60}^{∘}$ are special, and their trigonometric ratios have the particular values\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{0}^{∘}=0,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡{60}^{∘}=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{0}^{∘}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡{60}^{∘} & = \\\\ 0+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}+\\sqrt{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}. & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527941 1068 17592186527818 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527941 1069 "q-350328" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527941 1070 17592186045487 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527941 1071 "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=2{t}^{5}-4{t}^{3},\\,y={t}^{2},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nFind the Cartesian equation of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=1.$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of the tangent line is $y=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527941 1072 #uuid "52b01e00-7882-5db4-abc3-b8cfaaac3472" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527941 1074 "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2({1}^{5})-4({1}^{3})=2-4=-2 \\\\ y & ={1}^{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we're looking to find the equation of the tangent at the point $(-2,1).$ \n\nTo find the equation of the tangent line, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=10{t}^{4}-12{t}^{2},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2t\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2t}{10{t}^{4}-12{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t\\,=\\,1} & =\\frac{2(1)}{10({1}^{4})-12({1}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{10-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(-2)} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the Cartesian equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-2,1)$ and the slope $m=-1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =-1(x-(-2)) \\\\ y-1 & =-(x+2) \\\\ y & =-x-2+1 \\\\ y & =-x-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the equation of the tangent line  in slope-intercept form is $y=-x-1.$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527942 1068 17592186527801 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527942 1069 "q-218679" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527942 1070 17592186045487 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527942 1071 "![](images/38/386b2307d81c8f0aa895a192ac2734a7b24f122edb26229f3f9c10f0941c96e7.png)\n\nThe average rate of change of the curve shown above, over the interval $[0,4]$, is $\\frac{Δy}{Δx}=${{field-1}}." 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527942 1072 #uuid "5381d86b-69c1-53d6-ab4d-bc350eeb1469" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527942 1074 "From the graph, we can see that $f(0)=-2$ and $f(4)=1.$\n\nApplying the formula for the average rate of change, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δy}{Δx} & =\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(4)-f(0)}{4-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-(-2)}{4-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527943 1068 17592186527864 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527943 1069 "q-122584" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527943 1070 17592186045487 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527943 1071 "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{t+1},\\,y={t}^{3}+4,\\,-1<t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nCalculate the equation of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=1.$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527943 1072 #uuid "5579ca0e-f803-5d9f-87c0-8c8636fd4e81" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527943 1074 "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\sqrt{1+1}=\\sqrt{2}, \\\\ y & =(1)^{3}+4=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent at the point $(\\sqrt{2},5).$ \n\nTo find the equation of the tangent line, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t+1}},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t+1}})} \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}\\sqrt{(t+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=1} & =6\\cdot {1}^{2}\\sqrt{1+1} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(\\sqrt{2},5)$ and the slope $m=6\\sqrt{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-5 & =6\\sqrt{2}(x-\\sqrt{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527944 1068 17592186527816 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527944 1069 "q-122948" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527944 1070 17592186045485 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527944 1071 "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=1-{e}^{3t},\\,y=4{t}^{3},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of the parameter $t$ at which the normal line to the curve is vertical." 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527944 1072 #uuid "5a6c8612-f46a-5c05-98fb-598338ec8310" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527944 1074 "The normal line is vertical when the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-3{e}^{3t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=12{t}^{2}\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n12{t}^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,t=0.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(0)=-3{e}^{3(0)}=-3\\ne 0,\\,\\,\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=0.$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527945 1068 17592186527874 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527945 1069 "q-94084" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527945 1070 17592186045487 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527945 1071 "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the elliptic cone ${x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=8{z}^{2}$ and the line $\\frac{x}{4}=\\frac{y}{2}=z-1.$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527945 1072 #uuid "5a9b6c0a-2d84-570a-8860-6a6e61ceeed3" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527945 1074 "First, we write the equation of our line in parametric form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{4}=\\frac{y}{2}=z-1=t\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=4t \\\\ y=2t \\\\ z=t+1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSubstituting the above expressions for $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ into the equation of the elliptic cone, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2} & =8{z}^{2} \\\\ (4t)^{2}+4(2t)^{2} & =8(t+1)^{2} \\\\ 16{t}^{2}+16{t}^{2} & =8(t+1)^{2} \\\\ 32{t}^{2} & =8(t+1)^{2} \\\\ 4{t}^{2} & =(t+1)^{2} \\\\ 4{t}^{2} & ={t}^{2}+2t+1 \\\\ 3{t}^{2}-2t-1 & =0 \\\\ (3t+1)(t-1) & =0 \\\\ t & =-\\frac{1}{3},1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=-\\frac{1}{3},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=4(-\\frac{1}{3})=-\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ y=2(-\\frac{1}{3})=-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ z=-\\frac{1}{3}+1=\\frac{2}{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(-\\frac{4}{3},-\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{2}{3}).$\n\n- For $t=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=4(1)=4 \\\\ y=2(1)=2 \\\\ z=1+1=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(4,2,2).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(-\\frac{4}{3},-\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{2}{3})$ and $(4,2,2).$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527946 1068 17592186527813 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527946 1069 "q-122836" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527946 1070 17592186045485 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527946 1071 "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{4}+2{t}^{2},\\,y={t}^{3}-3t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of the parameter $t$ at which the normal line to the curve is horizontal." 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527946 1072 #uuid "5b067330-3793-583e-b08f-f7fd948895ab" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527946 1074 "The normal line is horizontal when the tangent line to the curve is vertical. Therefore, we require ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${y}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=4{t}^{3}+4t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}-3\n$$\n\nSolving ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n4t({t}^{2}+1)=0\\,⟹\\,t=0.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(0)=3(0)^{2}-3=-3\\ne 0,\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed horizontal when $t=0.$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527947 1068 17592186527871 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527947 1069 "q-161546" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527947 1070 17592186045485 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527947 1071 "Consider the type II region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,(3y-z)^{2}\\le x\\le {e}^{y+4z}\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is some finite region in the $yz$-plane. Then,\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V=\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527947 1072 #uuid "5bfcca82-6474-54bc-a5e0-2e4323bbf96b" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527947 1074 "A type II region in three-dimensional space can be written as\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,{u}_{1}(y,z)\\le x\\le {u}_{2}(y,z)\\}.\n$$\n\nA typical three-dimensional type II region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/1b/1b49704bf6d5837a18d8b5dbb8b420d430d5e3881507540fb62a45cf1f6ce52f.png)\n\n \n\nIf $R$ is a type II region, we can express the triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over $R$ as a mixed integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x]\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(y,z)=(3y-z)^{2}\\,{u}_{2}(y,z)={e}^{y+4z}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our triple integral can be written as\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{(3y-z)^{2}}^{{e}^{y+4z}}f(x,y,z)\\,dx]dA.\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527948 1068 17592186527859 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527948 1069 "q-50863" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527948 1070 17592186045485 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527948 1071 "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{3n}{x}^{n-1}.\n$$\n\nCalculate the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor series expansion about $x=0$ of ${f}^{′}(x).$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527948 1072 #uuid "5c748f35-86fe-5e16-bc4c-e8e0b39f4472" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527948 1074 "Writing the first few terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{3n}{x}^{n-1}=-\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{6}x-\\frac{1}{9}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{12}{x}^{3}-⋯.\n$$\n\nTo find ${f}^{′}(x),$ we differentiate term-by-term. This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=0+\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{2}{9}x+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}-⋯.\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527949 1068 17592186527848 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527949 1069 "q-126170" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527949 1070 17592186045485 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527949 1071 "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(3x)^{n}}{{2}^{n+1}}.\n$$\n\nThe series converges to $f(x)$ for $|x|<R,$ where $R$ is the radius of convergence of the Taylor series. Calculate the radius of convergence of the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ about $x=0.$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527949 1072 #uuid "5f964668-eee6-5cc5-b2ad-92e74506589f" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527949 1074 "The radius of convergence for the Taylor series of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is the same as the Taylor series of $f(x).$ So, to find the radius of convergence of ${f}^{′}(x),$ we need to compute $R.$\n\nTo calculate $R,$ we can use the ratio test. Computing the limit of the absolute value of the ratio of two consecutive terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(3x)^{n+1}}{{2}^{n+2}}\\cdot \\frac{{2}^{n+1}}{(3x)^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{3x}{2}| \\\\  & =\\frac{3|x|}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe series converges when $L<1.$ So, it converges when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3|x|}{2} & <1 \\\\ |x| & <\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of convergence is $R=\\frac{2}{3}.$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527950 1068 17592186527835 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527950 1069 "q-146208" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527950 1070 17592186045487 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527950 1071 "$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,8)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0.45, & y=4 \\\\ 0.55, & y=6 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSuppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Find the conditional variance $\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=8]$ given that the conditional probability mass function ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,8)$ is shown above and the corresponding conditional expected value is ${\\mu }_{Y|8}=5.1.$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527950 1072 #uuid "611fada0-4ebb-55c6-bcaf-8dccb0c75bca" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527950 1074 "Recall that the conditional variance of $Y$ given $X=x$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=x] & =\\text{E}[(Y-{\\mu }_{Y|x})^{2}\\,|\\,X=x] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}\\,|\\,X=x]-{\\mu }_{Y|x}^{2} \\\\  & =[{\\sum }_{y}{y}^{2}\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)]-{\\mu }_{Y|x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=8] & =[{\\sum }_{y}{y}^{2}\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,8)]-{\\mu }_{Y|8}^{2} \\\\  & =[(4)^{2}\\cdot 0.45+(6)^{2}\\cdot 0.55]-(5.1)^{2} \\\\  & =7.2+19.8-26.01 \\\\  & =0.99.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527951 1068 17592186527904 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527951 1069 "q-73867" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527951 1070 17592186045487 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527951 1071 "For the elliptic paraboloid $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{3}=z+4$, the section in the plane $\\sqrt{3}x-2y=4$ is a parabola. \nFind the vertex of the projection of this parabola onto $yz$-plane." 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527951 1072 #uuid "65227920-db32-5f4f-9774-747e0bc8ffd1" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527951 1074 "Since we want to find the projection onto the $yz$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $x.$ First, we solve for $x$ in the equation of the plane, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3}x-2y=4\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{2(y+2)}{\\sqrt{3}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $x$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{(\\frac{2(y+2)}{\\sqrt{3}})}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{3} & =z+4 \\\\ \\frac{4(y+2)^{2}}{3\\cdot 2}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{3} & =z+4 \\\\ \\frac{2(y+2)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{3} & =z+4 \\\\ \\frac{3(y+2)^{2}}{3} & =z+4 \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =z+4 \\\\ z & =(y+2)^{2}-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the vertex of the projection onto $yz$-plane is $(-2,-4).$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527952 1068 17592186527882 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527952 1069 "q-347643" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527952 1070 17592186045487 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527952 1071 "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=-2$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-1)^{n+1}\\frac{{3}^{n}}{n}(x+2)^{n}.\n$$\n\nFind the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }$\n{{field-1}}$(-1)^{n+1}:(x+2)^{n-1}$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527952 1072 #uuid "68b82aa8-1837-5666-8db7-ce28e32f59f7" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527952 1074 "Writing the terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=3(x+2)-\\frac{9}{2}(x+2)^{2}+⋯+{{(-1)^{n+1}\\frac{{3}^{n}}{n}(x+2)^{n}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above series term-by-term gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=3-9(x+2)+⋯+{{(-1)^{n+1}{3}^{n}(x+2)^{n-1}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\n$$\n\nWe can now write the Taylor series for ${f}^{′}(x)$ using the sigma notation as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{3}^{n}(-1)^{n+1}(x+2)^{n-1}\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527953 1068 17592186527841 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527953 1069 "q-4761" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527953 1070 17592186045485 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527953 1071 "![](images/44/4456324cb31f62f0d856bc71b0f0a1e8f68e2b0ae0a8e9148e4d4dde9c4aca7d.png)\n\n$\\operatorname{sin}⁡H=$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527953 1072 #uuid "6daa4c32-448d-5d51-8b4c-73d314338ef5" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527953 1074 "We have that $m∠H={45}^{∘},$ which is a special angle. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡H & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527954 1068 17592186527828 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527954 1069 "q-347640" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527954 1070 17592186045485 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527954 1071 "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n}{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nCalculate the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor series expansion about $x=0$ of ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$-\\,{x}^{3}+\\,⋯$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527954 1072 #uuid "6f7b083c-c283-53ee-9bad-76a4fd42d736" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527954 1074 "Writing the first few terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n}{x}^{n}=x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}+⋯.\n$$\n\nTo find ${f}^{′}(x),$ we differentiate term-by-term. This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=1-x+{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}+⋯.\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527955 1068 17592186527893 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527955 1069 "q-113810" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527955 1070 17592186045485 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527955 1071 "The point $P$ with coordinates $(3,-2)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x).$ Which point must lie on $y=-f(x)?$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527955 1072 #uuid "735fa5c9-8ac9-5976-8041-9e59cf3d3694" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527955 1074 "The transformation $y=-f(x)$ reflects the curve $y=f(x)$ in the $x$-axis.\n\nFirst, notice that the point $P(3,-2)$ lies $2$ units *below* the $x$-axis. So, under the action of the transformation, this point will be reflected $2$ units *above* the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/c2/c20856218b6f5f18c9a3465027a8f1a64a67f2c0d8ef3bd97fba5236f7b81bab.png)\n\nWe conclude that the point $(3,2)$ must lie on the graph of $y=-f(x).$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527956 1068 17592186527854 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527956 1069 "q-83470" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527956 1070 17592186045487 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527956 1071 "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨4{e}^{2t},\\,9{e}^{3t}⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $(1,1)$ and has velocity $⟨2,4⟩$ when $t=0,$ calculate the position vector of $P$ at time $t.$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527956 1072 #uuid "743918b2-4124-5be2-a1fd-716500b8cb35" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527956 1074 "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector. To find the velocity vector, we first need to integrate the acceleration vector. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨4{e}^{2t},\\,9{e}^{3t}⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 4{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int 9{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{e}^{2t}+{C}_{1},\\,3{e}^{3t}+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(0)=⟨2,4⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =⟨2{e}^{2(0)}+{C}_{1},\\,3{e}^{3(0)}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,4⟩ & =⟨2+{C}_{1},3+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=0$ and ${C}_{2}=1.$ Therefore, the velocity vector is\n\n$$\nv(t)=⟨2{e}^{2t},\\,3{e}^{3t}+1⟩.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the position vector by integrating the velocity vector.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨2{e}^{2t},\\,3{e}^{3t}+1⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 2{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int (3{e}^{3t}+1)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{2t}+{C}_{3},\\,{e}^{3t}+t+{C}_{4}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(0)=⟨1,1⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨{e}^{2(0)}+{C}_{3},\\,{e}^{3(0)}+0+{C}_{4}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,1⟩ & =⟨1+{C}_{3},\\,1+{C}_{4}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{3}={C}_{4}=0.$ Therefore, the position of $P$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{e}^{2t},\\,{e}^{3t}+t⟩.\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527957 1068 17592186527826 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527957 1069 "q-161772" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527957 1070 17592186045485 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527957 1071 "Evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}xyz\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the type III region given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 2,\\,0\\le x\\le \\sqrt{4-2z},\\,0\\le y\\le x\\}.\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527957 1072 #uuid "7a83145c-370c-5913-b3c6-439072b6c354" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527957 1074 "Writing down our triple integral as a repeated integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}xyz\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-2x}}{\\int }_{0}^{x}xyz\\,dy\\,dx\\,dz.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $y$, treating $x$ and $z$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-2x}}{\\int }_{0}^{x}xyz\\,dy\\,dx\\,dz & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-2x}}[{\\int }_{0}^{x}xyz\\,dy]dx\\,dz \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-2x}}xz{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=x}dx\\,dz \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-2x}}xz(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})\\,dx\\,dz \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-2x}}{x}^{3}z\\,dx\\,dz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $x$, treating $z$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-2x}}{x}^{3}z\\,dx\\,dz & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}z[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-2z}}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}z{[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}]}_{x=0}^{x=\\sqrt{4-2z}}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{4}(4-2z)^{2}z\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}4z-4{z}^{2}+{z}^{3}\\,\\text{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}4z-4{z}^{2}+{z}^{3}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\frac{1}{2}{[2{z}^{2}-\\frac{4}{3}{z}^{3}+\\frac{1}{4}{z}^{4}]}_{z=0}^{z=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(8-\\frac{32}{3}+4)-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527958 1068 17592186527814 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527958 1069 "q-50330" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527958 1070 17592186045487 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527958 1071 "![](images/31/31d4236bf58cd579c49c7259c32a2a9171e07c771b4c34040ac92896809f695f.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $x=\\frac{2}{y}$ and $x=3-y$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=-2.$ Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527958 1072 #uuid "7bcaaf90-d33e-5a2f-9583-78877f436c71" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527958 1074 "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{y} & =3-y \\\\ 2 & =3y-{y}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2}-3y+2 & =0 \\\\ (y-1)(y-2) & =0 \\\\ y & =1,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, our limits of integration are from $y=1$ to $y=2.$\n\nNow, let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/b7/b73c341f4d09b1df7bea162bc7dbe10d0ee60920c847f9c24210386a3d139e61.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x=\\frac{2}{y},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $x=3-y.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x=3-y.$\n\n![](images/c8/c8c8e766c66e47a54f316f54893d603b245acec8f529da3a5033d2bba547a319.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}[\\,(3-y)-(-2)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}{[5-y]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x=\\frac{2}{y}.$\n\n![](images/1b/1b31331f1c0da353bee6fe29d10e9e58dd531cbc8152cbce214d1adea771b032.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}{[\\,(\\frac{2}{y})-(-2)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}{[\\frac{2}{y}+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}{[5-y]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}{[\\frac{2}{y}+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}({[5-y]}^{2}-{[\\frac{2}{y}+2]}^{2})\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527959 1068 17592186527878 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527959 1069 "q-28653" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527959 1070 17592186045486 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527959 1071 "$5{cos}^{2}⁡{45}^{∘}+3{sin}^{2}⁡{45}^{∘}-7{tan}^{2}⁡{45}^{∘}=$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527959 1072 #uuid "7bdb766f-cf3d-53c5-a841-7b6efda706a9" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527959 1074 "The angle ${45}^{∘}$ is special, and its trigonometric ratios have the values\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡{45}^{∘}=1,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡{45}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nAlso, note that ${sin}^{2}⁡{45}^{∘}$ means $(\\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘})^{2},$ ${cos}^{2}⁡{45}^{∘}$ means $(\\operatorname{cos}⁡{45}^{∘})^{2}$ and  ${tan}^{2}⁡{45}^{∘}$ means $(\\operatorname{tan}⁡{45}^{∘})^{2}.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{cos}^{2}⁡{45}^{∘}+3{sin}^{2}⁡{45}^{∘}-7{tan}^{2}⁡{45}^{∘} & = \\\\ 5(\\operatorname{cos}⁡{45}^{∘})^{2}+3(\\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘})^{2}-7(\\operatorname{tan}⁡{45}^{∘})^{2} & = \\\\ 5{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}+3{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}-7(1)^{2} & = \\\\ 8{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}-7 & = \\\\ 8(\\frac{2}{4})-7 & = \\\\ 4-7 & = \\\\ -3. & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527960 1068 17592186527809 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527960 1069 "q-21129" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527960 1070 17592186045487 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527960 1071 "The ice level in the North Pole is decreasing due to global warming. The thickness of ice, measured in feet, in the North Pole, is represented by the expression $135+0.1x-0.02{x}^{2},$ where $x$ is the time, in years, since measurements began. What is the average rate of decrease of the ice thickness over the first $10$ years?" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527960 1072 #uuid "7c25ff0b-f786-5f82-b2d5-6e5163b97efc" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527960 1074 "Let $f(x)=135+0.1x-0.02{x}^{2}$ be the thickness of the ice, in feet, after $x$ years. To find the average rate of change of the ice thickness over the first $10$ years, we use the average rate of change formula on the interval $[0,10]$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δx} & =\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(10)-f(0)}{10-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{[135+0.1(10)-0.02(10)^{2}]-[135+0.1(0)-0.02(0)^{2}]}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{134-135}{10} \\\\  & =-0.1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the average rate of decrease of the ice thickness over the first $10$ years is $0.1$ feet per year." 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527961 1068 17592186527857 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527961 1069 "q-75975" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527961 1070 17592186045485 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527961 1071 "Find the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ then what is the value of ${x}_{2}?$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527961 1072 #uuid "7c3f2557-93da-5d6b-97fe-29c0805f0db0" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527961 1074 "Remember, the order of the vectors in a basis is important.\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}={{(\\,6\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{{(\\,-3\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx=6\\cdot {b}_{1}+(-3)\\cdot {b}_{2}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we have that ${x}_{2}=-3.$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527962 1068 17592186527806 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527962 1069 "q-161689" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527962 1070 17592186045487 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527962 1071 "Evaluate the mixed integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}5yz\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}A\n$$\n\ngiven that the region $D$ in the $xz$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 2,\\,0\\le x\\le 2-z\\}.\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527962 1072 #uuid "7dfdc43a-549b-5350-87ba-ec6dde24e325" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527962 1074 "First, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $y,$ treating $z$ and $x$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}5yz\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}5z{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{∬}_{D}z(4-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type II region in the $xz$-plane.\nTherefore, to evaluate our double integral as a repeated integral over this region, we integrate with respect to $x$ and then with respect to $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2}{∬}_{D}z(4-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & =\\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2-z}z(4-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}z[{\\int }_{0}^{2-z}(4-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}z{[(4x-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})]}_{x=0}^{x=2-z}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}z(4(2-z)-\\frac{(2-z)^{3}}{3})\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{16z}{3}-2{z}^{3}+\\frac{{z}^{4}}{3})\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{[\\frac{8{z}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{z}^{4}}{2}+\\frac{{z}^{5}}{15}]}_{z=0}^{z=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}[\\frac{32}{3}-8+\\frac{32}{15}] \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527963 1068 17592186527870 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527963 1069 "q-146272" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527963 1070 17592186045486 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527963 1071 "| $f$ | $Y=4$ | $Y=8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $\\frac{1}{16}$ | $\\frac{3}{16}$ |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n\nSuppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Find $\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=4]$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above and the corresponding conditional expected value is ${\\mu }_{X|4}=\\frac{4}{5}.$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527963 1072 #uuid "7e4093f7-c4fd-5107-9969-358e958506e1" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527963 1074 "Recall that the conditional variance of $X$ given $Y=y$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=y] & =\\text{E}[(X-{\\mu }_{X|y})^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2} \\\\  & =[{\\sum }_{x}{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order to find ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y),$ let's first find the marginal distribution of $Y,$ corresponding to the column totals: \n\n| $f$ | $Y=4$ | $Y=8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $\\frac{1}{16}$ | $\\frac{3}{16}$ |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $\\frac{5}{16}$ | $\\frac{11}{16}$ |\n\nThe conditional probability mass function of $X$ given that $Y=y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, dividing each joint probability by the corresponding column total, we obtain the conditional probability mass function of $X$ given that $Y=y:$\n\n| ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ | $Y=4$ | $Y=8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ | $\\frac{3}{11}$ |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{4}{5}$ | $\\frac{8}{11}$ |\n|  | $1$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=4] & =[{\\sum }_{x}{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,4)]-{\\mu }_{X|4}^{2} \\\\  & =[{0}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}+{1}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{4}{5}]-{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =0+\\frac{4}{5}-\\frac{16}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527964 1068 17592186527873 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527964 1069 "q-10207" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527964 1070 17592186045486 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527964 1071 "![](images/d4/d4bcdb6515b262c070c7ed689263efbbde8a350ad6a28b4fc106de281e69ff46.png)\n\nFind the area bounded between the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2t,\\,y=t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis for $t\\in [0,\\pi ].$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527964 1072 #uuid "8222fa21-5fa3-52f1-a894-4b100aeb534d" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527964 1074 "The unsigned area bounded by the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=x(t),\\,y=y(t),\\,{t}_{1}\\le t\\le {t}_{2}\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis is given by the absolute value of the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the integral expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this integral will give us a positive number since $y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\ge 0$ for all $t\\in [0,\\pi ].$\n\nTo solve the above integral, we use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & ⇒ & v=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =2t(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }+2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }+2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-2(\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -0\\operatorname{cos}⁡0)+2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =-2\\pi (-1) \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527965 1068 17592186527812 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527965 1068 17592186527815 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527965 1069 "q-350393" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527965 1070 17592186045486 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527965 1071 "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0<t<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ on the curve. Determine the coordinates of the point at which the normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis. Do not round the answers.\n\nThe normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis at $(${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527965 1072 #uuid "8516a769-888c-5185-902e-1a5250ccaa04" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527965 1074 "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=3\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}=\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we're looking to determine the equation of the normal at the point $(\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cot}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t\\,=\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}} & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m:$\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})}=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}}=\\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=\\sqrt{3}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-\\frac{1}{2} & =\\sqrt{3}(x-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\ y-\\frac{1}{2} & =\\sqrt{3}x-\\frac{3(3)}{2} \\\\ y & =\\sqrt{3}x-\\frac{9}{2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y & =\\sqrt{3}x-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe normal crosses the $y$-axis when $x=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis at  $(0,\\,-4).$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527966 1068 17592186527843 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527966 1069 "q-146204" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527966 1070 17592186045485 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527966 1071 "| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,4)$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{5}{8}$ |\n\nSuppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Find $\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=4]$ given that the conditional probability mass function ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,4)$ is shown in the table above and the corresponding conditional expected value is ${\\mu }_{X|4}=3.$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527966 1072 #uuid "86c9830c-c21b-52dd-a632-55f72d300bd2" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527966 1074 "Recall that the conditional variance of $X$ given $Y=y$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=y] & =\\text{E}[(X-{\\mu }_{X|y})^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2} \\\\  & =[{\\sum }_{x}{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=4] & =[{\\sum }_{x}{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,4)]-{\\mu }_{X|4}^{2} \\\\  & =[(0)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{8}+(2)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}+(4)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{5}{8}]-(3)^{2} \\\\  & =0+1+10-9 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527967 1068 17592186527868 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527967 1069 "q-84409" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527967 1070 17592186045487 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527967 1071 "Kevin is moving his car in a straight line. His position relative to a fixed origin $O$ is given by the function $x(t)=2{e}^{t/2}\\,\\text{m},$ where $t$ is the time in seconds. Find its average position between $2$ and $6$ seconds." 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527967 1072 #uuid "881af2f1-aaf5-5e5d-a46a-df43ce3ea5ee" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527967 1074 "To calculate the average value of the position on $[{t}_{1},{t}_{2}],$ we use the average value formula\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{avg}}=\\frac{1}{{t}_{2}-{t}_{1}}\\,{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{6-2}\\,{\\int }_{2}^{6}2{e}^{t/2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {2\\cdot 2({e}^{t/2})|}_{2}^{6} \\\\  & ={e}^{6/2}-{e}^{2/2} \\\\  & ={e}^{3}-e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the average position is $({e}^{3}-e)\\,\\text{m}.$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527968 1068 17592186527811 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527968 1069 "q-146246" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527968 1070 17592186045487 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527968 1071 "| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $\\frac{1}{16}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $X=4$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{3}{16}$ |\n| $X=6$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ |\n\nSuppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Find $\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=6]$ given that $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above and the corresponding conditional expected value is ${\\mu }_{Y|6}=3.$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527968 1072 #uuid "8a520314-a8ce-5660-9be7-ab082651f66c" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527968 1074 "Recall that the conditional variance of $Y$ given $X=x$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=x] & =\\text{E}[(Y-{\\mu }_{Y|x})^{2}\\,|\\,X=x] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}\\,|\\,X=x]-{\\mu }_{Y|x}^{2} \\\\  & =[{\\sum }_{y}{y}^{2}\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)]-{\\mu }_{Y|x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order to find ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x),$ let's first find the marginal distribution for $X,$ corresponding to the row totals: \n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | ${f}_{X}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $\\frac{1}{16}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{5}{16}$ |\n| $X=4$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{3}{16}$ | $\\frac{7}{16}$ |\n| $X=6$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n\nThe conditional probability mass function of $Y$ given that $X=x$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, dividing each joint probability by the corresponding row total, we obtain the conditional probability mass function of $Y$ given that $X=x:$\n\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ | $\\frac{4}{5}$ | $1$ |\n| $X=4$ | $\\frac{4}{7}$ | $\\frac{3}{7}$ | $1$ |\n| $X=6$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=6] & =[{\\sum }_{y}{y}^{2}\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,6)]-{\\mu }_{Y|6}^{2} \\\\  & =[{2}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+{4}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}]-{3}^{2} \\\\  & =2+8-9 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527969 1068 17592186527888 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527969 1069 "q-76022" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527969 1070 17592186045485 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527969 1071 "The point $P$ with coordinates $(3,5)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x).$ Which point must lie on $y=-f(x)?$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527969 1072 #uuid "8d4620e5-facf-5ccf-9ec9-900f97e30c4c" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527969 1074 "The transformation $y=-f(x)$ reflects the curve $y=f(x)$ in the $x$-axis.\n\nFirst, notice that the point $P(3,5)$ lies $5$ units *above* the $x$-axis. So, under the action of the transformation, this point will be reflected $5$ units *below* the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/15/15c5cadbce652b40c2507fe9457ba9d836239c63e232e4924fe03a1615e76817.png)\n\nWe conclude that the point $(3,-5)$ must lie on the graph of $y=-f(x).$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527970 1068 17592186527883 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527970 1069 "q-79877" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527970 1070 17592186045487 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527970 1071 "![](images/49/49b119b378ac4f45c0c395ca77c5cefead3a7c811295b51ac8eaa8aef7100e25.png)\n\nThe base of a solid is the region enclosed by the curves $y=1-{x}^{2}$ and $y=-1,$  as shown in the picture. Cross-sections perpendicular to the $x$-axis are rectangles. If the height of each rectangle is $2$ times the length of its base, which integral gives the volume of the solid?" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527970 1072 #uuid "8deca7b4-0aaf-504b-958b-1e6e90c74a93" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527970 1074 "Let $f(x)=1-{x}^{2}$ and $g(x)=-1.$\n\nFirst, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the two curves by setting the functions equal to one another and solving for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ 1-{x}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limit of integration are $x=-\\sqrt{2}$ and $x=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nNow, consider a cross-section at some point $x$, where $x\\in (-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$,  and its projection on the $xy$-plane: \n\n![](images/0c/0c2a676ecc781a2a7a78870eb93ee9fbe5a89a7bd754a446231c6e3237f75831.png)\n\nThe base of the cross-section is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =|f(x)-g(x)| \\\\  & =|1-{x}^{2}-(-1)| \\\\  & =|2-{x}^{2}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of a rectangle is\n$A=bh.$\n  So, since we have $h=2b,$ the expression for the area of the cross-section is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(x) & =bh \\\\  & =2{b}^{2} \\\\  & =2{(2-{x}^{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =8-8{x}^{2}+2{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now calculate the volume of the solid, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}A(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}(8-8{x}^{2}+2{x}^{4})\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527971 1068 17592186527863 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527971 1069 "q-75267" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527971 1070 17592186045487 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527971 1071 "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=2{t}^{2}+8t,\\,y=4{t}^{7},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nCalculate the equation of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=1.$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527971 1072 #uuid "9473c03d-7209-5d2d-815a-9982b2c23f32" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527971 1074 "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2(1)^{2}+8(1)=2+8=10, \\\\ y & =4(1)^{7}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent at the point $(10,4).$ \n\nTo find the equation of the tangent line, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=4t+8,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=28{t}^{6}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{28{t}^{6}}{4t+8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=1} & =\\frac{28\\cdot (1)^{6}}{4\\cdot (1)+8} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(10,4)$ and the slope $m=\\frac{7}{3}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-4 & =\\frac{7}{3}(x-10).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527972 1068 17592186527850 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527972 1069 "q-350384" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527972 1070 17592186045487 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527972 1071 "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=2{t}^{5}-4{t}^{3},\\,y={t}^{2},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=1$ on the curve. Find the Cartesian equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of the normal line at $C$ is $y=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527972 1072 #uuid "947f538f-26d4-542e-9645-e3e57363dc38" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527972 1074 "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2({1}^{5})-4({1}^{3})=2-4=-2 \\\\ y & ={1}^{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $(-2,1).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=10{t}^{4}-12{t}^{2},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2t\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2t}{10{t}^{4}-12{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t\\,=\\,1} & =\\frac{2(1)}{10({1}^{4})-12({1}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{10-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(-2)} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m:$\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{\\,(-1)\\,}=1\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the Cartesian equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-2,1)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =1(x-(-2)) \\\\ y-1 & =x+2 \\\\ y & =x+2+1 \\\\ y & =x+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the equation of the normal line  in slope-intercept form is $y=x+3.$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527973 1068 17592186527851 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527973 1069 "q-126105" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527973 1070 17592186045487 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527973 1071 "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=2$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{(2n-1)!}(x-2)^{2n}.\n$$\n\nCalculate the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor series expansion about $x=2$ of ${f}^{′}(x).$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527973 1072 #uuid "98bdadc3-a767-57cf-925f-bd9117f593a8" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527973 1074 "Writing the first few terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{(2n-1)!}(x-2)^{2n}=-(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{1}{6}(x-2)^{4}-\\frac{1}{120}(x-2)^{6}+⋯.\n$$\n\nTo find ${f}^{′}(x),$ we differentiate term-by-term. This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-2(x-2)+\\frac{2}{3}(x-2)^{3}-\\frac{1}{20}(x-2)^{5}+⋯.\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527974 1068 17592186527886 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527974 1069 "q-163509" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527974 1070 17592186045486 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527974 1071 "![](images/33/335bf5e4e2a4ee9d0286eadb968e0e82c6afb18d47cd5258d6d1b6cee3d5c5a6.png)\n\nGiven that $R$ is the type III region shown above, \n${∭}_{R}xz\\,\\text{d}V=$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527974 1072 #uuid "9c0ba772-2d81-508b-bca4-4e2298a7113a" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527974 1074 "For a type III region $R$ with projection $D$ onto the $xz$-plane, the triple integral of a continuous function $f(x,y,z)$ over $R$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,z)}^{{u}_{2}(x,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe consider the region $R$ as a type III region enclosed between the back surface $y={u}_{1}(x,z)=1$ and the front surface $y={u}_{2}(x,z)=3.$\n\n![](images/b1/b176bcf502a0801eae2bf470dfe4bf92852a34b06675fd74be78b30ccb4f6469.png)\n\nSo, the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,1\\le y\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ as a type I *plane* region in the $xz$-plane with the upper curve\n\n$$\nz=1-{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\n![](images/eb/eb054b5f1567aa2c56842d79133fbbefc41a8b40730c688381d88e3ef06ccab7.png)\n\nHence, $D=\\{(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le 1-{x}^{2}\\}.$\n\nTherefore, we write the triple integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}xz\\,\\text{d}V & ={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{1}^{3}xz\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-{x}^{2}}[{\\int }_{1}^{3}xz\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-{x}^{2}}xz[y]_{y=1}^{y=3}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-{x}^{2}}2xz\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527975 1068 17592186527803 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527975 1069 "q-274929" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527975 1070 17592186045485 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527975 1071 "Given that $P$ is a $2\\times 5$ matrix, $Q$ is a $5\\times 7$ matrix, and $R$ is a $7\\times 2$ matrix, in which of the following expressions are the Hadamard products well-defined?\n\nI. $PQ⊙R$\n\nII. $PQ⊙{R}^{T}$\n\nIII. $(PQ)^{T}⊙R$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527975 1072 #uuid "9c36f01c-b274-57ce-a233-5c5a47d4ef94" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527975 1074 "The Hadamard product of two matrices (or vectors) is only defined if they have the same dimensions.\n\nLet's consider each expression in turn:\n\n- The dimensions of $PQ$ are $2\\times 7,$ and the dimensions of $R$ are $7\\times 2.$ Hence, the Hadamard product in expression I is *not* well-defined.\n\n- The dimensions of $PQ$ are $2\\times 7,$ and the dimensions of ${R}^{T}$ are $2\\times 7.$ Hence, the Hadamard product in expression II is well-defined.\n\n- The dimensions of $(PQ)^{T}$ are $7\\times 2,$ and the dimensions of $R$ are $7\\times 2.$ Hence, the Hadamard product in expression III is well-defined.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527976 1068 17592186527804 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527976 1069 "q-146202" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527976 1070 17592186045485 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527976 1071 "$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,1)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0.4, & x=1 \\\\ 0.6, & x=2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSuppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Find the conditional variance $\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=1]$ given that the conditional probability mass function ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,1)$ is shown above and the corresponding conditional expected value is ${\\mu }_{X|1}=1.6.$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527976 1072 #uuid "9f6a4dc1-cc0e-50e8-a876-123c929a6728" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527976 1074 "Recall that the conditional variance of $X$ given $Y=y$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=y] & =\\text{E}[(X-{\\mu }_{X|y})^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2} \\\\  & =[{\\sum }_{x}{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=y] & =[{\\sum }_{x}{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,1)]-{\\mu }_{X|1}^{2} \\\\  & =[(1)^{2}\\cdot 0.4+(2)^{2}\\cdot 0.6]-(1.6)^{2} \\\\  & =0.4+2.4-2.56 \\\\  & =0.24.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527977 1068 17592186527833 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527977 1069 "q-113802" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527977 1070 17592186045485 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527977 1071 "![](images/4d/4ddca862c34122937a111162c54146169e4421291e587d1c9425f82db8b1c194.png)\n\nThe function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following gives the plot of $y=-f(x)?$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527977 1072 #uuid "a2dc0eae-2d75-5993-9bdd-983fb1853b64" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527977 1074 "To obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)$ from $y=f(x)$, we need to reflect the graph over the $x$-axis. The resulting graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/84/84d0887ee0913eac98dbe7b86229386ce439986fc00754e6ad43e8feede6943e.png)" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527978 1068 17592186527875 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527978 1069 "q-12738" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527978 1070 17592186045485 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527978 1071 "![](images/ae/ae54b87e2d9fe596b2042b6ddd77ca622e703d580d8facedd5b18df8ebfca1e7.png)\n\nThe function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following gives the plot of $y=-f(x)?$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527978 1072 #uuid "a3b63338-dbf9-5694-b2da-0ac508216008" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527978 1074 "To obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)$ from $y=f(x)$, we need to reflect the graph over the $x$-axis. The resulting graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/c9/c948864e1705fccb9407f70d2a9694e1b4607c9ceefeea9250b2c876396ce005.png)" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527979 1068 17592186527885 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527979 1069 "q-75268" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527979 1070 17592186045485 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527979 1071 "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={e}^{-t},\\,y=5{t}^{2}+1,\\,0\\le t\\le 2.\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of the parameter $t$ at which the normal line to the curve is vertical." 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527979 1072 #uuid "ab667ef1-091e-5d1a-aa96-b7c759cdc55d" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527979 1074 "The normal line is vertical when the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-{e}^{-t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=10t\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n10t=0\\,⟹\\,t=0.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(0)=-{e}^{-0}=-1\\ne 0,\\,\\,\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=0.$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527980 1068 17592186527867 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527980 1069 "q-124172" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527980 1070 17592186045487 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527980 1071 "![](images/09/09a38bb4306728f97b7fecbf9ab1bda7aa957607352c1ab27af09489c271c3ed.png)\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}({y}^{2}-2x)\\,\\text{d}y,$ where $C$ is the path traversed along the line segment from the point $(0,1)$ to the point $(2,3),$ as shown above." 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527980 1072 #uuid "ada3d128-ac86-51c2-88c8-3e672e4cad88" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527980 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a line segment with endpoints $(0,1)$ and $(2,3).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨(0+t(2-0)),\\,(1+t(3-1))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,\\,1+2t⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+2t)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={y}^{2}-2x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(1+2t)^{2}-2(2t) \\\\  & =1+4t+4{t}^{2}-4t \\\\  & =4{t}^{2}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4{t}^{2}+1)\\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4{t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{[\\frac{4{t}^{3}}{3}+t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2(\\frac{4}{3}+1)-2(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527981 1068 17592186527849 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527981 1069 "q-94951" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527981 1070 17592186045487 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527981 1071 "Which of the following best describes the projection of the intersection between the elliptic cone $(x-2)^{2}+2(y+1)^{2}=2{z}^{2}$ and the plane $x+2z-3=0$ onto the $yz$-plane?" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527981 1072 #uuid "b35b0faf-35a9-5d63-9b1f-dccf9512ee2b" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527981 1074 "Since we want to find the projection onto the $yz$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $x.$ First, we solve the equation of the plane for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx+2z-3=0\\,⟹\\,x=3-2z\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $x$ into the equation of the elliptic cone, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3-2z-2)^{2}+2(y+1)^{2} & =2{z}^{2} \\\\ (1-2z)^{2}+2(y+1)^{2} & =2{z}^{2} \\\\ 1-4z+4{z}^{2}+2(y+1)^{2} & =2{z}^{2} \\\\ 2{z}^{2}-4z+1+2(y+1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2(z-1)^{2}-2+1+2(y+1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2(z-1)^{2}+2(y+1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ (y+1)^{2}+(z-1)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the equation of a circle of radius $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ centered at $(-1,1)$ in the $yz$-plane." 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527982 1068 17592186527860 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527982 1069 "q-161578" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527982 1070 17592186045485 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527982 1071 "Evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}2\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the type II region given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,0\\le x\\le 4-y-z\\}.\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527982 1072 #uuid "b38931ca-6fb1-5b3c-b019-b45d2fe482a7" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527982 1074 "Writing down our triple integral as a repeated integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}2\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4-y-z}2\\,dx\\,dz\\,dy.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $x,$ treating $y$ and $z$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4-y-z}2\\,dx\\,dz\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{4-y-z}2\\,dx]dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[2x]_{x=0}^{x=4-y-z}dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2(4-y-z)\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}8-2y-2z\\,dz\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z,$ treating $y$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}8-2y-2z\\,dz\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}8-2y-2z\\,dz]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[8z-2yz-{z}^{2}]}_{z=0}^{z=1}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}8-2y-1\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}7-2y\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}7-2y\\,dy & ={[7y-{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =7-1 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527983 1068 17592186527861 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527983 1069 "q-352615" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527983 1070 17592186045485 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527983 1071 "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\sqrt{5n+2}\\,(-4x)^{n}.\n$$\n\nThe series converges to $f(x)$ for $|x|<R,$ where $R$ is the radius of convergence of the Taylor series. Calculate the radius of convergence of the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ about $x=0.$\n\nThe radius of convergence of the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527983 1072 #uuid "b3dadad8-be2e-5983-9aeb-5a80535c1cf7" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527983 1074 "The radius of convergence for the Taylor series of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is the same as the Taylor series of $f(x).$ So, to find the radius of convergence of ${f}^{′}(x),$ we need to compute $R.$\n\nTo calculate $R,$ we can use the ratio test. Computing the limit of the absolute value of the ratio of two consecutive terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{\\sqrt{5(n+1)+2}\\,(-4x)^{n+1}}{\\sqrt{5n+2}\\,(-4x)^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{\\sqrt{5n+7}}{\\sqrt{5n+2}}||(-4x)| \\\\  & =4|x|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe series converges when $L<1.$ So, it converges when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4|x| & <1 \\\\ |x| & <\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of convergence of the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is $R=\\frac{1}{4}.$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527984 1068 17592186527825 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527984 1069 "q-101739" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527984 1070 17592186045485 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527984 1071 "Find the interval where the average rate of change of $f(x)$ equals $0$ given the following table of values of $f(x):$\n\n| $x$ | $\\,-6\\,$ | $\\,-4\\,$ | $\\,-2\\,$ | $\\,0\\,$ | $\\,2\\,$ | $\\,4\\,$ | $\\,6\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\,-7\\,$ | $\\,-5\\,$ | $\\,2\\,$ | $\\,-2\\,$ | $\\,5\\,$ | $\\,-5\\,$ | $\\,-8\\,$ |" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527984 1072 #uuid "b5d8a95f-b00e-51c0-82a6-3182a1f44e55" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527984 1074 "We have $\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=0$ if and only if $Δf=0.$ So\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & Δf=0 \\\\  & f(b)-f(a)=0 \\\\  & f(b)=f(a).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means we have to find an interval  $[a,b]$ such that $f(a)=f(b).$\n\nSince $f(-4)=f(4)=-5,$ we conclude that $\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=0$ on the interval $[-4,4].$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527985 1068 17592186527837 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527985 1069 "q-21120" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527985 1070 17592186045487 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527985 1071 "The height $h(t),$ measured in feet, of a plant after $t$ years is given by the expression $h(t)=3{t}^{3/2}-2t.$ What is the average rate of growth over the first $4$ years?" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527985 1072 #uuid "b6dc0dd6-8281-5909-b3f4-b0dcdc2e7ecf" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527985 1074 "Let $h(t)=3{t}^{3/2}-2t,$ the height measured in feet, of a plant after $t$ years. To find the average rate of change of $h(t)$ from $t=0$ to $t=4,$ we use the average rate of change formula on the interval $[0,4]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δh}{Δt} & =\\frac{h(b)-h(a)}{b-a} \\\\  & =\\frac{h(4)-h(0)}{4-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{[3({4}^{3/2})-2(4)]-[3({0}^{3/2})-2(0)]}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{24-8}{4} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the average rate of growth is $4$ feet per year." 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527986 1068 17592186527810 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527986 1069 "q-69666" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527986 1070 17592186045485 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527986 1071 "Find the interval where the average rate of change of $f(x)$ equals $0$ given the following table of values of $f(x):$\n\n| $x$ | $\\,-3\\,$ | $\\,-1\\,$ | $\\,0\\,$ | $\\,1\\,$ | $\\,3\\,$ | $\\,5\\,$ | $\\,7\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\,1\\,$ | $\\,3\\,$ | $\\,-1\\,$ | $\\,-5\\,$ | $\\,7\\,$ | $\\,3\\,$ | $\\,-4\\,$ |" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527986 1072 #uuid "b8be6849-5f2a-595e-8c72-25b5d6a335d3" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527986 1074 "We have $\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=0$ if and only if $Δf=0.$ So\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & Δf=0 \\\\  & f(b)-f(a)=0 \\\\  & f(b)=f(a).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means we have to find an interval  $[a,b]$ such that $f(a)=f(b).$\n\nSince $f(-1)=f(5)=3,$ we conclude that $\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=0$ on the interval $[-1,5].$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527987 1068 17592186527879 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527987 1069 "q-19157" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527987 1070 17592186045487 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527987 1071 "The table below lists values of $f(x)$ at distinct $x$-values. Knowing that the average rate of change of the function $f(x)$ on the interval $[2p,2]$ is $3,$ find the value of $p.$ \n\n| $x$ | $\\,-9\\,$ | $\\,-4\\,$ | $\\,2p\\,$ | $\\,-1\\,$ | $\\,0\\,$ | $\\,1\\,$ | $\\,2\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\,-12\\,$ | $\\,-8\\,$ | $\\,-4\\,$ | $\\,-3\\,$ | $\\,2\\,$ | $\\,6\\,$ | $\\,8\\,$ |" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527987 1072 #uuid "bc7ad0f1-6b84-5b13-8e8e-b050839dc127" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527987 1074 "Using the formula for the average rate of change, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δx} & =\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(2)-f(2p)}{2-2p} \\\\  & =\\frac{8-(-4)}{2-2p} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{2-2p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we know that $\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=3$ on the interval $[2p,2].$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\frac{12}{2-2p}=3.\n$$\n\nSolving for $p,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12}{2-2p} & =3 \\\\ 12 & =3\\cdot (2-2p) \\\\ 12 & =6-6p \\\\ 12-6 & =-6p \\\\ 6 & =-6p \\\\ \\frac{6}{-6} & =\\frac{-6p}{-6} \\\\ -1 & =p.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $p=-1.$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527988 1068 17592186527845 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527988 1069 "q-161572" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527988 1070 17592186045487 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527988 1071 "Evaluate the mixed integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{1-y-z}24y\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ in the $yz$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{1}{2},\\,0\\le z\\le y\\}.\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527988 1072 #uuid "bf9108b7-225d-5060-8e6a-417eee5fc631" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527988 1074 "First, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $x,$ treating $y$ and $z$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{1-y-z}24y\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}24y[x]_{x=0}^{x=1-y-z}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}24y(1-y-z)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}24y-24{y}^{2}-24yz\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the double integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}24y-24{y}^{2}-24yz\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{\\int }_{0}^{y}24(y-{y}^{2})-24yz\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}24(y-{y}^{2})[z]_{z=0}^{z=y}-12y[{z}^{2}]_{z=0}^{z=y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}24(y-{y}^{2})y-12{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}24{y}^{2}-36{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[8{y}^{3}-9{y}^{4}]}_{y=0}^{y=1/2} \\\\  & =8(\\frac{1}{8}-0)-9(\\frac{1}{16}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527989 1068 17592186527831 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527989 1069 "q-122700" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527989 1070 17592186045486 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527989 1071 "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}+3t,\\,y=1+\\sqrt{2t},\\,t\\in [0,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=2$ on the curve. Determine the point at which the tangent to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis." 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527989 1072 #uuid "c3009bb8-76e4-5e7a-be7f-b556ddcfdaef" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527989 1074 "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=2$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(2)^{2}+3(2)=10, \\\\ y & =1+\\sqrt{2(2)}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(10,3).$ \n\nTo find the equation of the tangent line, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2t+3,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2t}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2t}}}{2t+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2t}(2t+3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=2,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=2} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\cdot 2}(2\\cdot 2+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(10,3)$ and the slope $m=\\frac{1}{14}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-3 & =\\frac{1}{14}(x-10).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe tangent crosses the $y$-axis when $x=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-3 & =\\frac{1}{14}(0-10) \\\\ y-3 & =-\\frac{5}{7} \\\\ y & =\\frac{16}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $(0,\\,\\frac{16}{7})$ is the $y$-intercept." 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527990 1068 17592186527865 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527990 1069 "q-76758" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527990 1070 17592186045485 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527990 1071 "$\\operatorname{sin}⁡{0}^{∘}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}=$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527990 1072 #uuid "c582da59-32d5-5633-9b42-0c1f59213de1" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527990 1074 "The angles ${0}^{∘}$ and ${30}^{∘}$ are special angles, and their trigonometric ratios have the values\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{0}^{∘}=0,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{0}^{∘}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘} & = \\\\ 0+\\frac{1}{2} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}. & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527991 1068 17592186527856 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527991 1069 "q-143431" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527991 1070 17592186045487 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527991 1071 "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2}$ of two pineapples is drawn from a population. The population has pineapples of two different weights, $1\\,\\text{kg}$ and $1.5\\,\\text{kg},$ distributed in the ratio $1:4.$ Calculate the standard error of $\\overset{―}{X}.$ Round your answer to two decimal places." 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527991 1072 #uuid "c60b1a3b-1eee-5254-929b-6a532b619fd6" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527991 1074 "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]}=\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the pineapples in the population have weights $1\\,\\text{kg}$ and $\\text{1.5}\\,\\text{kg},$ and these are distributed in the ratio $1:4.$ Therefore, to compute the population's mean and variance, we can consider a population consisting of $1+4=5$ pineapples only with the following weights:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\text{kg},\\,1.5\\,\\text{kg},\\,1.5\\,\\text{kg},\\,1.5\\,\\text{kg},\\,1.5\\,\\text{kg}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(1+4\\cdot 1.5) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(1+6) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{5} \\\\  & =1.4\\,\\text{kg},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}^{2}-{\\mu }^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}({1}^{2}+4\\cdot {1.5}^{2})-{1.4}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(10)-1.96 \\\\  & =2-1.96 \\\\  & =0.04\\,{\\text{kg}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the sample size is $n=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}=\\frac{0.04}{2}=0.02\\,{\\text{kg}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{0.02}≈0.14\\,\\text{kg}.\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527992 1068 17592186527895 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527992 1069 "q-124246" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527992 1070 17592186045485 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527992 1071 "![](images/88/88da0f1ddf1f605186c9d921a9eade24b5f0aa9de2ae4afdc51a921586093b16.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x,$ where $C$ is the path along the line segment from the point $(-2,3)$ to the point $(0,0),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨2t-2,\\,3-3t⟩.$*" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527992 1072 #uuid "ca5fbd9d-06c2-5516-a2aa-caaa1b2a1fc6" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527992 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a line segment with endpoints $(-2,3)$ and $(0,0).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨(-2+t(0-(-2))),\\,(3+t(0-3))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t-2,\\,3-3t⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-2)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(2t-2)^{2}(3-3t) \\\\  & =4(t-1)^{2}\\cdot 3(1-t) \\\\  & =-12(t-1)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}-12(t-1)^{3}\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-24{\\int }_{0}^{1}(t-1)^{3}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527993 1068 17592186527907 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527993 1068 17592186527909 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527993 1069 "q-350343" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527993 1070 17592186045486 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527993 1071 "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}t+\\frac{3}{2},\\,y={t}^{3}+7{t}^{2}+8t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=-1$ on the curve. Determine the coordinates of the point at which the tangent to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis. Do not round the answers.\n\nThe tangent to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis at $(${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527993 1072 #uuid "cdc93f1c-a598-5c34-91ec-6e0adc895b1e" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527993 1074 "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=-1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(-1)^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}(-1)+\\frac{3}{2}=1-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}=2 \\\\ y & =(-1)^{3}+7(-1)^{2}+8(-1)=-1+7-8=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(2,-2).$ \n\nTo find the equation of the tangent line, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2t+\\frac{1}{2},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}+14t+8\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{t}^{2}+14t+8}{2t+\\frac{1}{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=-1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t\\,=\\,-1} & =\\frac{3(-1)^{2}+14(-1)+8}{2(-1)+\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{(-\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(2,-2)$ and the slope $m=2.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(-2) & =2(x-2) \\\\ y+2 & =2x-4 \\\\ y & =2x-4-2 \\\\ y & =2x-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe tangent crosses the $y$-axis when $x=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2(0)-6 \\\\ y & =0-6 \\\\ y & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the tangent to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis at  $(0,\\,-6).$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527994 1068 17592186527906 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527994 1069 "q-96454" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527994 1070 17592186045487 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527994 1071 "Calculate $\\int \\frac{{tanh}^{2}⁡(3x-2)}{3}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527994 1072 #uuid "cef928b3-a5b9-5b14-83bc-17bf537996f2" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527994 1074 "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the secant-tangent identity \n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic secant-tangent identity:\n\n$$\n1-{tanh}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sech}^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nSetting $\\theta =3x-2,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-{tanh}^{2}⁡(3x-2) & ={sech}^{2}⁡(3x-2) \\\\ {tanh}^{2}⁡(3x-2) & =1-{sech}^{2}⁡(3x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{tanh}^{2}⁡(3x-2)}{3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int (1-{sech}^{2}⁡(3x-2))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int 1\\cdot \\text{d}x-\\frac{1}{3}\\int {sech}^{2}⁡(3x-2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(3x-2)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527995 1068 17592186527819 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527995 1069 "q-120180" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527995 1070 17592186045487 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527995 1071 "Consider the finite region bounded by the curve $y=\\sqrt{x+2}$ and the $y$-axis from $y=2$ to $y=3.$ Which integral gives the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527995 1072 #uuid "d18dd5ed-e185-5cfc-926d-0d7b2a92c6df" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527995 1074 "The volume of the solid generated when the region bounded by a curve $x=g(y),$ the $y$-axis, and the horizontal lines $y=c$ and $y=d$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}[g(y)]^{2}\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write our equation in terms of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{x+2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =x+2 \\\\ x & ={y}^{2}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in our example, $g(y)={y}^{2}-2$ and the limits of integration are from $y=2$ to $y=3.$\n\nFinally, the volume can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}{[g(y)]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{3}{({y}^{2}-2)}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{3}({y}^{4}-4{y}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527996 1068 17592186527892 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527996 1069 "q-161723" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527996 1070 17592186045485 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527996 1071 "Consider the type III region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nS=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 2+{z}^{2}\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is some finite region in the $xz$-plane. Then,\n\n$$\n{∭}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V=\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527996 1072 #uuid "d33bfb77-09ea-5195-a418-173b3296a490" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527996 1074 "A type III region in three-dimensional space can be written as\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,{u}_{1}(x,z)\\le y\\le {u}_{2}(x,z)\\}.\n$$\n\nA typical three-dimensional type III region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/06/0603bed12c6af74e46544d8bebc52362aed95a9a5ad7418ea4faaf228f3b5241.png)\n\n \n\nIf $R$ is a type III region, we can express the triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over $R$ as a mixed integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,z)}^{{u}_{2}(x,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y]\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x,z)={x}^{2},\\,{u}_{2}(x,z)=2+{z}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our triple integral can be written as\n\n$$\n{∭}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{2+{z}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,dy]dA.\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527997 1068 17592186527847 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527997 1069 "q-161562" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527997 1070 17592186045487 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527997 1071 "The triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{∗}^{∗}{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{∗}^{∗}f(x,y,z)\\,dx\\,dy\\,dz\n$$\n\nis defined over the type II region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-2\\le y\\le z,\\,2\\le z\\le 3,\\,-y-z\\le x\\le {y}^{2}+{z}^{4}\\}.\n$$\n\nWhat is $a+b?$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527997 1072 #uuid "d3a2b2c0-5dc8-56bd-94aa-3a2ccfd9aff3" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527997 1074 "A type II region in three-dimensional space can be written as\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,{u}_{1}(y,z)\\le x\\le {u}_{2}(y,z)\\}.\n$$\n\nA typical three-dimensional type II region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/a9/a905e1f60fb0aa865c071fcbccbfecb30dcac7a74b4b647d24a40f4c89ff1935.png)\n\n \n\nIf $R$ is a type II region, we can express the triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over $R$ as a mixed integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x]\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(y,z)=-y-z,\\,{u}_{2}(y,z)={y}^{2}+{z}^{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our triple integral can be written as\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{-y-z}^{{y}^{2}+{z}^{4}}f(x,y,z)\\,dx]dA.\n$$\n\nNow, the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le y\\le z,\\,2\\le z\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by expressing the double integral over $D$ as a repeated integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{-y-z}^{{y}^{2}+{z}^{4}}f(x,y,z)\\,dx]dA & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}{\\int }_{-2}^{z}[{\\int }_{-y-z}^{{y}^{2}+{z}^{4}}f(x,y,z)\\,dx]dy\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}{\\int }_{-2}^{z}{\\int }_{-y-z}^{{y}^{2}+{z}^{4}}f(x,y,z)\\,dx\\,dy\\,dz.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b=-2+z=z-2.\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527998 1068 17592186527838 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527998 1069 "q-50865" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527998 1070 17592186045485 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527998 1071 "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{n}}{4n}.\n$$\n\nCalculate the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor series expansion about $x=0$ of ${f}^{′}(x).$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527998 1072 #uuid "d411bca7-2808-5bc3-9792-16db8d0254d6" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527998 1074 "Writing the first few terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{n}}{4n}=\\frac{x}{4}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{12}+⋯.\n$$\n\nTo find ${f}^{′}(x),$ we differentiate term-by-term. This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{x}{4}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+⋯.\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527999 1068 17592186527900 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527999 1069 "q-126164" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527999 1070 17592186045486 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527999 1071 "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2n+1}}{n}.\n$$\n\nFind ${a}_{n},$ given that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}.\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527999 1072 #uuid "d825b7e3-8340-5978-bde4-a68ed2d3d297" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186527999 1074 "Writing the terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{3}+\\frac{{x}^{5}}{2}+⋯+{{\\frac{{x}^{2n+1}}{n}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above series term-by-term gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}+\\frac{5{x}^{4}}{2}+⋯+{{\\frac{(2n+1){x}^{2n}}{n}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the Taylor series for ${f}^{′}(x)$ using the sigma notation as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(2n+1){x}^{2n}}{n}\n$$\n\nComparing the series above with the given series, we can deduce that\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{(2n+1){x}^{2n}}{n}.\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528000 1068 17592186527908 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528000 1069 "q-350373" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528000 1070 17592186045487 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528000 1071 "A curve is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx={t}^{3}-6{t}^{2}+12t,\\,y=4{t}^{2}-3t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is vertical. Do not round the answer.\n\nThe tangent to the curve is vertical at $t=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528000 1072 #uuid "d888db88-904a-5f76-93b9-4129d0fd45c9" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528000 1074 "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is vertical if ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${y}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}-12t+12,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=8t-3\n$$\n\nSolving ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{t}^{2}-12t+12 & =0 \\\\ 3({t}^{2}-4t+4) & =0 \\\\ 3{(t-2)}^{2} & =0\\,⟹\\,t=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(2) & =8(2)-3\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the tangent to the curve is vertical at $t=2.$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528001 1068 17592186527890 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528001 1069 "q-2448" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528001 1070 17592186045485 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528001 1071 "A curve is defined as $y=3x-5.$ Find the average rate of change of $y$ over the interval $[3,7].$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528001 1072 #uuid "dba8d021-2c20-52de-b1fb-abd8fe5f7a55" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528001 1074 "Let $f(x)=3x-5.$ Applying the formula for the average rate of change, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δy}{Δx} & =\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(7)-f(3)}{7-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{[3(7)-5]-[3(3)-5]]}{7-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{16-4}{7-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{4} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528002 1068 17592186527830 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528002 1069 "q-15939" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528002 1070 17592186045485 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528002 1071 "Find the average rate of change of the function $f(x)$ on the interval $[-3,1]$ given the following table of values for $f(x):$\n\n| $x$ | $\\,-3\\,$ | $\\,-2\\,$ | $\\,-1\\,$ | $\\,0\\,$ | $\\,1\\,$ | $\\,2\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\,2\\,$ | $\\,-2\\,$ | $\\,1\\,$ | $\\,2\\,$ | $\\,-3\\,$ | $\\,-3\\,$ |" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528002 1072 #uuid "dc25546e-e94f-545f-9768-cf226be3dc76" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528002 1074 "Using the formula for the averge rate of change, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δx} & =\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(1)-f(-3)}{1-(-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3-2}{1-(-3)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528003 1068 17592186527901 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528003 1069 "q-73332" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528003 1070 17592186045486 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528003 1071 "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{1}^{2}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528003 1072 #uuid "dc80cc6c-733b-50ec-85a0-e9f5b6f29bef" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528003 1074 "Let $u={x}^{2}-1.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x\\,⟹\\,x\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\text{d}u}{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get\n\n| **$x$** | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **$u$** | $0$ | $3$ |\n\nWe can now solve the integral using our new variable $u,$ remembering to use the new limits of integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}\\cdot x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{(\\frac{2{u}^{3/2}}{3})|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & ={\\frac{{u}^{3/2}}{3}|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{3}^{3/2}}{3}-\\frac{{0}^{3/2}}{3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528004 1068 17592186527808 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528004 1069 "q-161724" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528004 1070 17592186045485 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528004 1071 "Consider the type III region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nS=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,{x}^{2}-2\\le y\\le {x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is some finite region in the $xz$-plane. Then,\n\n$$\n{∭}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V=\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528004 1072 #uuid "e1798463-dc83-5fa2-a428-d476acd2c869" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528004 1074 "A type III region in three-dimensional space can be written as\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,{u}_{1}(x,z)\\le y\\le {u}_{2}(x,z)\\}.\n$$\n\nA typical three-dimensional type III region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/06/0603bed12c6af74e46544d8bebc52362aed95a9a5ad7418ea4faaf228f3b5241.png)\n\n \n\nIf $R$ is a type III region, we can express the triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over $R$ as a mixed integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,z)}^{{u}_{2}(x,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y]\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x,z)={x}^{2}-2,\\,{u}_{2}(x,z)={x}^{2}+{z}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our triple integral can be written as\n\n$$\n{∭}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{x}^{2}-2}^{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,dy]dA.\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528005 1068 17592186527827 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528005 1069 "q-161580" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528005 1070 17592186045487 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528005 1071 "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}2xyz\\,dV\n$$\n\nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,0\\le x\\le y+z\\},\n$$\n\nand $D$ is a finite region in $yz$-plane enclosed between the $y$-axis, the line $y=1$ and the line $z=y$ in the first quadrant." 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528005 1072 #uuid "e31a3154-dd92-51e0-baa5-7e556d0b4865" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528005 1074 "Writing down our triple integral as a mixed integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}2xyz\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{y+z}2xyz\\,dx]dA.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $x,$ treating $y$ and $z$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{y+z}2xyz\\,dx]dA & ={∬}_{D}yz[{x}^{2}]_{x=0}^{x=y+z}\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}yz(y+z)^{2}\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}yz({y}^{2}+2yz+{z}^{2})\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{3}z+2{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+y{z}^{3}\\,dA\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now evaluate the double integral above over the region $D$ in the $yz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/59/5974f852ba8dcaf9e6c4d6323709987c590e2f739524e012545738bc4bb842b0.png)\n\nWe can evaluate our double integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{y}^{3}z+2{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+y{z}^{3}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{y}{y}^{3}z+2{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+y{z}^{3}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{y}{y}^{3}z+2{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+y{z}^{3}\\,dz]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{3}{z}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{2}{z}^{3}+\\frac{1}{4}y{z}^{4}]}_{z=0}^{z=y}dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{3}\\cdot {y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{2}\\cdot {y}^{3}+\\frac{1}{4}y\\cdot {y}^{4}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{17}{12}{y}^{5}\\,dy \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{12}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{5}\\,dy \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{12}{[\\frac{1}{6}{y}^{6}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{12}(\\frac{1}{6}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{72}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528006 1068 17592186527846 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528006 1069 "q-161738" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528006 1070 17592186045485 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528006 1071 "Evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}3x\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the type III region given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le x,\\,0\\le z\\le 2-x\\}.\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528006 1072 #uuid "e4e7d822-5d73-50ee-b641-1c594f9a070a" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528006 1074 "Writing down our triple integral as a repeated integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}3x\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2-x}{\\int }_{0}^{x}3x\\,dy\\,dz\\,dx.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $y$, treating $x$ and $z$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2-x}{\\int }_{0}^{x}3x\\,dy\\,dz\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2-x}[{\\int }_{0}^{x}3x\\,dy]dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2-x}3x[y]_{y=0}^{y=x}dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2-x}3x(x-0)\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2-x}3{x}^{2}\\,dz\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z$, treating $x$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2-x}3{x}^{2}\\,dz\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}3{x}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2-x}\\,\\text{d}z]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}3{x}^{2}{[z]}_{z=0}^{z=2-x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}3{x}^{2}(2-x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(6{x}^{2}-3{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(6{x}^{2}-3{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x & ={[2{x}^{3}-\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{4}]}_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =(16-12)-0 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528007 1068 17592186527897 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528007 1069 "q-352513" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528007 1070 17592186045487 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528007 1071 "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=2$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(x-2)^{n}}{3n+4}.\n$$\n\nFind the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }$\n{{field-1}}\n$(-1)^{n+1}(x-2)^{n-1}$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528007 1072 #uuid "e8579e15-923e-57b1-875d-4092d7ca1f68" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528007 1074 "Writing the terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{x-2}{7}-\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{10}+⋯+{{\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(x-2)^{n}}{3n+4}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above series term-by-term gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{7}-\\frac{2(x-2)}{10}+⋯+{{\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}n(x-2)^{n-1}}{3n+4}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\n$$\n\nWe can now write the Taylor series for ${f}^{′}(x)$ using the sigma notation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n(-1)^{n+1}(x-2)^{n-1}}{3n+4} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n}{3n+4}(-1)^{n+1}(x-2)^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528008 1068 17592186527894 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528008 1069 "q-161745" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528008 1070 17592186045487 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528008 1071 "$$\n{∭}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{a}^{∗}{\\int }_{∗}^{∗}{\\int }_{∗}^{b}f(x,y,z)\\,dy\\,dz\\,dx\n$$\n\nThe triple integral above is defined over the type III region given by\n\n$$\nS=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,-2\\le z\\le {x}^{2}+2,\\,-2-z\\le y\\le 2+x+z\\}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $a-b?$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528008 1072 #uuid "ea162333-68ea-5f39-92a3-425dd5b76bfd" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528008 1074 "A type III region in three-dimensional space can be written as\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,{u}_{1}(x,z)\\le y\\le {u}_{2}(x,z)\\}.\n$$\n\nA typical three-dimensional type III region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/06/0603bed12c6af74e46544d8bebc52362aed95a9a5ad7418ea4faaf228f3b5241.png)\n\n \n\nIf $R$ is a type III region, we can express the triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over $R$ as a mixed integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,z)}^{{u}_{2}(x,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y]\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x,z)=-2-z,\\,{u}_{2}(x,z)=2+x+z.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our triple integral can be written as\n\n$$\n{∭}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{-2-z}^{2+x+z}f(x,y,z)\\,dy]dA.\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,-2\\le z\\le {x}^{2}+2\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by expressing the double integral over $D$ as a repeated integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{-2-z}^{2+x+z}f(x,y,z)\\,dy]dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-2}^{{x}^{2}+2}[{\\int }_{-2-z}^{2+x+z}f(x,y,z)\\,dy]dz\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-2}^{{x}^{2}+2}{\\int }_{-2-z}^{2+x+z}f(x,y,z)\\,dy\\,dz\\,dx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na-b=(0)-(2+x+z)=-2-x-z.\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528009 1068 17592186527872 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528009 1069 "q-83878" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528009 1070 17592186045485 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528009 1071 "The rate of growth in the frog population $P(t)$ in a certain lake follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.1P(5\\,000-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years. Find an expression for the frog population after $t$ years, given that there were $100$ frogs initially." 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528009 1072 #uuid "ea94a739-28fe-50a1-b5d4-4f34307441cf" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528009 1074 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.1,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=5\\,000.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{5\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-(5\\,000\\cdot 0.1)t}}=\\frac{5\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-500t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=100$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-500\\cdot 0}} & =100 \\\\ 1+C & =\\frac{5\\,000}{100} \\\\ C & =50-1 \\\\  & =49.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{5\\,000}{1+49{e}^{-500t}}.\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528010 1068 17592186527840 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528010 1069 "q-121766" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528010 1070 17592186045485 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528010 1071 "$\\operatorname{sin}⁡{60}^{∘}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡{90}^{∘}=$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528010 1072 #uuid "eba5d927-837f-59e6-9d7f-e021bd02e879" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528010 1074 "The angles ${60}^{∘}$ and ${90}^{∘}$ are special angles, and their trigonometric ratios have values\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{60}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡{90}^{∘}=0.\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{60}^{∘}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡{90}^{∘} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}-0 & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}. & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528011 1068 17592186527899 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528011 1069 "q-161690" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528011 1070 17592186045486 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528011 1071 "![](images/ca/ca994203c4a3889949e3452f48ea3f7295a152c5d9d4c809758f152d7115cedf.png)\n\nGiven that $R$ is the type II region shown above, \n${∭}_{R}2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,\\text{d}V=$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528011 1072 #uuid "f2d33e33-cbe1-5877-8ed6-72bcb8e7fff5" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528011 1074 "For a type II region $R$ with projection $D$ onto the $yz$-plane, the triple integral of a continuous function $f(x,y,z)$ over $R$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe consider the region $R$ as a type II region enclosed between the back surface $x={u}_{1}(y,z)=1$ and the front surface $x={u}_{2}(y,z)=2.$\n\n![](images/a3/a39d7a27f20283b6e6814db926801f16e3e2cb42c7fe16487350b536d3b63d3c.png)\n\nSo, the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,1\\le x\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ as a type I *plane* region in the $yz$-plane with the upper curve\n\n$$\nz=1-(y-1)^{2}.\n$$\n\n![](images/50/5058493860a1c4d919e3cd6786bb1aca156ae83d47f766c087e351e9764929c4.png)\n\nHence, $D=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,0\\le z\\le 1-(y-1)^{2}\\}.$\n\nTherefore, we write the triple integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,\\text{d}V & ={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{1}^{2}2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1-(y-1)^{2}}[{\\int }_{1}^{2}2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1-(y-1)^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z[{x}^{2}]_{x=1}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1-(y-1)^{2}}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528012 1068 17592186527802 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528012 1069 "q-352604" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528012 1070 17592186045485 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528012 1071 "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-1)^{n}\\sqrt{n}{(\\frac{x}{6})}^{n}.\n$$\n\nThe series converges to $f(x)$ for $|x|<R,$ where $R$ is the radius of convergence of the Taylor series. Calculate the radius of convergence of the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ about $x=0.$\n\nThe radius of convergence of the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528012 1072 #uuid "f4bbe9de-e1d4-5432-b7cd-e16cf2a892fd" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528012 1074 "The radius of convergence for the Taylor series of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is the same as the Taylor series of $f(x).$ So, to find the radius of convergence of ${f}^{′}(x),$ we need to compute $R.$\n\nTo calculate $R,$ we can use the ratio test. Computing the limit of the absolute value of the ratio of two consecutive terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}\\sqrt{n+1}{(\\frac{x}{6})}^{n+1}}{(-1)^{n}\\sqrt{n}{(\\frac{x}{6})}^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{\\sqrt{n+1}}{\\sqrt{n}}||\\frac{x}{6}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|x|}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe series converges when $L<1.$ So, it converges when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{|x|}{6} & <1 \\\\ |x| & <6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of convergence of the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is $R=6.$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528013 1068 17592186527881 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528013 1069 "q-122928" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528013 1070 17592186045487 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528013 1071 "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,y=2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,0<t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=e$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528013 1072 #uuid "f508d724-e816-5421-91b6-b8baa491ba13" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528013 1074 "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=e$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡e=4, \\\\ y & =2e\\operatorname{ln}⁡e=2e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(4,2e).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{4}{t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+2\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+2}{(\\frac{4}{t})} \\\\  & =\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+t}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=e,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=e} & =\\frac{e\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+e}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{e+e}{2}=e\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{\\,e\\,}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(4,2e)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{\\,e\\,}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-2e & =(-\\frac{1}{\\,e\\,})(x-4) \\\\ y-2e & =-\\frac{1}{\\,e\\,}x+\\frac{4}{\\,e\\,} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{\\,e\\,}x+\\frac{2{e}^{2}+4}{\\,e\\,}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528014 1058 17592186528031 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528014 1062 #uuid "017b013a-5450-516d-883d-82ac20f6d367" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528014 1063 17592186368716 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528015 1058 17592186528022 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528015 1062 #uuid "130bb05a-f55b-5271-9ddc-91048c211b25" 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528015 1063 17592186371700 13194139535791 17592186045417 true] [17592186528016 1062 #uuid "240bde0a-edd2-5c94-9c50-9f441bdb08ef" 13194139535791 175921860454